C · adjacency lists · creation · L1
Concept: Allocate n head pointers, zeroed to NULL, and free every edge node before the array
Misconception: An adjacency list for n vertices needs n bytes of heads.
Explanation: The list is an array of n head pointers, one per vertex, each starting at NULL for an empty neighbor list. The array needs n * sizeof(pointer) bytes, not n bytes. Freeing walks each list and frees its nodes before freeing the head array, otherwise every edge leaks. Space is O(V + E).
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow):
void **alHeadsBad(size_t n) { return calloc(n, 1); }
Correct:
typedef struct AdjEdge { int to; struct AdjEdge *next; } AdjEdge;
typedef struct { AdjEdge **head; size_t n; } AdjList;
int alInit(AdjList *l, size_t n) {
  l->head = n != 0 ? calloc(n, sizeof *l->head) : NULL;
  l->n = l->head != NULL ? n : 0;
  return l->head != NULL;
}
void alFree(AdjList *l) {
  for (size_t u = 0; u < l->n; u++) {
    AdjEdge *e = l->head[u];
    while (e != NULL) { AdjEdge *nx = e->next; free(e); e = nx; }
  }
  free(l->head);
  l->head = NULL;
  l->n = 0;
}
Complexity: O(V)

