C · adjacency lists · insertion-and-deletion · L3
Concept: Remove an edge with a pointer to the link that points at it; never read a node after freeing it
Misconception: free(*pp) followed by *pp = (*pp)->next unlinks a node.
Explanation: Walk with a pointer to a pointer, so the head and an interior node are handled by the same code: when (*pp)->to matches, save the node, set *pp = node->next, then free the node. Freeing first and then reading the next pointer reads freed memory, which AddressSanitizer reports as a use-after-free. Removal is O(degree).
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: heap-use-after-free):
struct BadEdge { int to; struct BadEdge *next; };
void alRemoveBad(struct BadEdge **head, size_t u, int v) {
  struct BadEdge **pp = &head[u];
  while (*pp != NULL && (*pp)->to != v) pp = &(*pp)->next;
  if (*pp == NULL) return;
  free(*pp);
  *pp = (*pp)->next;
}
Correct:
int alRemove(AdjList *l, size_t u, size_t v) {
  if (u >= l->n || v >= l->n) return 0;
  AdjEdge **pp = &l->head[u];
  while (*pp != NULL && (*pp)->to != (int)v) pp = &(*pp)->next;
  if (*pp == NULL) return 0;
  AdjEdge *dead = *pp;
  *pp = dead->next;
  free(dead);
  return 1;
}
Complexity: O(deg)

