C · adjacency matrices · creation · L1
Concept: Allocate n * n cells for an adjacency matrix, and check that n * n does not overflow
Misconception: An adjacency matrix for n vertices needs n cells per row, so n bytes in total.
Explanation: The matrix has one cell for every ordered pair of vertices, so a flat array needs n * n cells and cell (u, v) is at u * n + v. Allocating only n cells makes every row after the first an out-of-bounds write. For large n the product n * n can wrap around to a small number, so check n <= SIZE_MAX / n before multiplying. Space is O(V^2).
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow):
unsigned char *amAllocBad(size_t n) { return calloc(n, 1); }
unsigned char *amAllocWrapBad(size_t n) { return calloc(n * n, 1); }
Correct:
typedef struct { unsigned char *a; size_t n; } AdjMatrix;
int amInit(AdjMatrix *g, size_t n) {
  g->a = NULL;
  g->n = 0;
  if (n == 0 || n > SIZE_MAX / n) return 0;
  g->a = calloc(n * n, 1);
  if (g->a == NULL) return 0;
  g->n = n;
  return 1;
}
void amFree(AdjMatrix *g) { free(g->a); g->a = NULL; g->n = 0; }
Complexity: O(V^2)

