C · adjacency matrices · insertion-and-deletion · L2
Concept: An undirected edge sets both cell (u, v) and cell (v, u); has-edge must check both indexes against n
Misconception: Setting cell (u, v) is enough for an undirected edge.
Explanation: The matrix of an undirected graph is symmetric, so adding the edge u-v sets two cells. If only one is set, the graph answers differently depending on the order of the arguments. Lookups and updates are O(1), but u and v must be below n or the index u * n + v leaves the array.
Incorrect:
void amAddUndirBad(unsigned char *a, size_t n, size_t u, size_t v) { a[u * n + v] = 1; }
Correct:
int amAdd(AdjMatrix *g, size_t u, size_t v, int directed) {
  if (u >= g->n || v >= g->n) return 0;
  g->a[u * g->n + v] = 1;
  if (!directed) g->a[v * g->n + u] = 1;
  return 1;
}
int amHas(const AdjMatrix *g, size_t u, size_t v) {
  if (u >= g->n || v >= g->n) return 0;
  return g->a[u * g->n + v];
}
int amRemove(AdjMatrix *g, size_t u, size_t v, int directed) {
  if (u >= g->n || v >= g->n) return 0;
  g->a[u * g->n + v] = 0;
  if (!directed) g->a[v * g->n + u] = 0;
  return 1;
}
Complexity: O(1)

