C · adjacency matrices · patterns · L4
Concept: Floyd-Warshall needs a finite INF and a guard; INT_MAX + INT_MAX overflows
Misconception: INT_MAX is a good value for infinity in a distance matrix.
Explanation: Floyd-Warshall relaxes d[i][j] with d[i][k] + d[k][j] for every k. When both terms are INT_MAX, the sum overflows int, which is undefined behaviour that UBSan reports. Use a smaller INF such as 10^9 together with a guard that skips pairs where either side is INF. The algorithm runs in O(V^3) time and O(V^2) space.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: signed integer overflow):
void floydBad(int d[4][4]) {
  for (int k = 0; k < 4; k++)
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)
      for (int j = 0; j < 4; j++)
        if (d[i][k] + d[k][j] < d[i][j]) d[i][j] = d[i][k] + d[k][j];
}
Correct:
#define AM_INF 1000000000
void floyd4(int d[4][4]) {
  for (int k = 0; k < 4; k++)
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
      if (d[i][k] == AM_INF) continue;
      for (int j = 0; j < 4; j++) {
        if (d[k][j] == AM_INF) continue;
        if (d[i][k] + d[k][j] < d[i][j]) d[i][j] = d[i][k] + d[k][j];
      }
    }
}
Complexity: O(V^3)

