C · array · equality · L2
Concept: a == b compares addresses; compare contents with memcmp or a loop
Misconception: Two arrays with the same elements are equal under ==.
Explanation: In an expression an array becomes a pointer to its first element, so a == b asks whether both are the same array in memory. Two separate arrays are never equal. gcc warns with -Warray-compare. Use memcmp(a, b, n * sizeof *a) for plain integers; do not use it for floats (padding, -0.0 and NaN) or structs with padding.
Incorrect:
int sameBad(void) {
  int a[3] = {1, 2, 3};
  int b[3] = {1, 2, 3};
  return a == b;
}
Correct:
int sameInts(const int *a, const int *b, size_t n) {
  return memcmp(a, b, n * sizeof *a) == 0;
}
Complexity: O(n)

