C · AVL trees · access · L2
Concept: Store each node's height and compute the balance factor as height(left) - height(right) with NULL counted as 0
Misconception: The balance factor can be read from the children directly.
Explanation: An AVL tree keeps the balance factor of every node in -1..1. Storing the height in each node makes the factor O(1), but a missing child must count as height 0; reading n->l->h on a leaf dereferences NULL. After any change below a node, recompute its height from its children.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: null pointer):
typedef struct ANode { int key, h; struct ANode *l, *r; } ANode;
int abalBad(const ANode *n) { return n->l->h - n->r->h; }
Correct:
typedef struct ANode { int key, h; struct ANode *l, *r; } ANode;
int ah(const ANode *n) { return n != NULL ? n->h : 0; }
void aupd(ANode *n) {
  int a = ah(n->l), b = ah(n->r);
  n->h = 1 + (a > b ? a : b);
}
int abal(const ANode *n) { return n != NULL ? ah(n->l) - ah(n->r) : 0; }
