C · binary trees · insertion-and-deletion · L2
Concept: Free a tree in post-order: children first, then the node itself
Misconception: Freeing the node first and then its children is the same as the other order.
Explanation: After free(t) the fields t->left and t->right no longer exist, so reading them is a use-after-free. Post-order frees both subtrees and only then the node. The recursion depth equals the tree height, so a very unbalanced tree needs an iterative free.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: heap-use-after-free):
typedef struct TNode { int val; struct TNode *left, *right; } TNode;
TNode *newNode(int v) {
  TNode *t = malloc(sizeof *t);
  if (t == NULL) return NULL;
  t->val = v;
  t->left = t->right = NULL;
  return t;
}
void treeFreeBad(TNode *t) {
  if (t == NULL) return;
  free(t);
  treeFreeBad(t->left);
  treeFreeBad(t->right);
}
Correct:
typedef struct TNode { int val; struct TNode *left, *right; } TNode;
TNode *newNode(int v) {
  TNode *t = malloc(sizeof *t);
  if (t == NULL) return NULL;
  t->val = v;
  t->left = t->right = NULL;
  return t;
}
void treeFree(TNode *t) {
  if (t == NULL) return;
  treeFree(t->left);
  treeFree(t->right);
  free(t);
}
Complexity: O(n)

