C · binary trees · complexity · L1
Concept: The height is one plus the larger of the two subtree heights; following only the left child measures one path
Misconception: The height of a tree is the length of its left spine.
Explanation: Height (here counted in nodes, so an empty tree is 0) needs both subtrees: 1 + max(height(left), height(right)). Following only left children gives 1 for a tree that leans right. The recursion visits each node once, O(n), and uses stack space equal to the height.
Incorrect:
typedef struct TNode { int val; struct TNode *left, *right; } TNode;
int treeHeightBad(const TNode *t) {
  if (t == NULL) return 0;
  return 1 + treeHeightBad(t->left);
}
Correct:
typedef struct TNode { int val; struct TNode *left, *right; } TNode;
int treeHeight(const TNode *t) {
  if (t == NULL) return 0;
  int l = treeHeight(t->left), r = treeHeight(t->right);
  return 1 + (l > r ? l : r);
}
Complexity: O(n)

