C · bit-fields · access · L2
Concept: You cannot take the address of a bit-field; pass its value or use a getter
Misconception: A pointer to a bit-field member can be passed to a function.
Explanation: A bit-field may start in the middle of a byte, so it has no address. &s.a is a compile error. Read the value into an ordinary variable, or write get and set functions that take the struct pointer.
Incorrect (does not compile: cannot take address of bit-field):
struct B { unsigned a : 3; };
unsigned *addrBad(struct B *b) { return &b->a; }
Correct:
struct B { unsigned a : 3; };
unsigned getA(const struct B *b) { return b->a; }
void setA(struct B *b, unsigned v) { b->a = v & 7u; }
Complexity: O(1)

