C · bit-fields · mental-model · L1
Concept: Bit-fields pack into storage units, so sizeof is not the number of bits divided by 8
Misconception: A struct with 1 + 3 + 4 bits of fields is exactly one byte.
Explanation: The declared type of a bit-field sets its storage unit: unsigned fields are packed into 4-byte units, so struct { unsigned a : 1, b : 3, c : 4; } is 4 bytes with gcc. Bit order, padding and straddling are implementation-defined, which makes bit-field layout unsuitable for file formats and network protocols.
Incorrect:
struct Flags { unsigned a : 1; unsigned b : 3; unsigned c : 4; };
size_t flagsBadSize(void) { return (1 + 3 + 4) / 8; }
Correct:
struct Flags { unsigned a : 1; unsigned b : 3; unsigned c : 4; };
size_t flagsSize(void) { return sizeof(struct Flags); }
Complexity: O(1)

