C · bit-fields · mutation · L2
Concept: Storing a value that does not fit keeps only the low bits; range-check before assigning
Misconception: Assigning 9 to a 3-bit field clamps it to 7.
Explanation: A bit-field has the width you declare. Assigning a larger value silently keeps the low bits, so 9 stored in three bits becomes 1. Check the range in a setter and report failure instead of wrapping.
Incorrect:
struct Packed { unsigned v : 3; };
unsigned setBad(struct Packed *p, unsigned v) {
  p->v = v;
  return p->v;
}
Correct:
struct Packed { unsigned v : 3; };
int setChecked(struct Packed *p, unsigned v) {
  if (v > 7u) return 0;
  p->v = v;
  return 1;
}
Complexity: O(1)

