C · Bloom filters · mental-model · L1
Concept: A Bloom filter is a bit array; set bits with 1u << (i % 32) because 1 << 31 overflows int
Misconception: In a uint32_t word array, w[i / 32] |= 1 << (i % 32) sets bit i.
Explanation: A Bloom filter stores m bits and answers membership by checking k of them. In 32-bit words, bit i lives in word i / 32 at position i % 32. The literal 1 has type int, and 1 << 31 is signed overflow, undefined behaviour that UBSan reports; the unsigned literal 1u makes the shift well defined. Setting and testing a bit are O(1).
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: left shift):
void bitSetBad(uint32_t *w, size_t i) { w[i / 32] |= 1 << (i % 32); }
Correct:
typedef struct { uint32_t *w; size_t m; unsigned k; } Bloom;
void bitSet(uint32_t *w, size_t i) { w[i / 32] |= 1u << (i % 32); }
int bitGet(const uint32_t *w, size_t i) { return (w[i / 32] >> (i % 32)) & 1u; }
int bloomInit(Bloom *b, size_t m, unsigned k) {
  b->w = m != 0 ? calloc((m + 31) / 32, sizeof *b->w) : NULL;
  b->m = b->w != NULL ? m : 0;
  b->k = k;
  return b->w != NULL && k != 0;
}
void bloomFree(Bloom *b) { free(b->w); b->w = NULL; b->m = 0; }
Complexity: O(1)

