C · Bloom filters · access · L2
Concept: Derive k indexes from two hashes as (h1 + i * h2) % m; reusing h1 for every probe makes k pointless
Misconception: The k probes can all use the same hash value.
Explanation: A Bloom filter needs k different bit positions per key. Instead of k hash functions, compute two (DJB2 as h1, FNV-1a with the low bit forced to 1 as h2) and use h1 + i * h2 for probe i. If every probe uses h1 % m, all k positions are the same bit and the filter behaves as if k were 1. Hashing is O(length of the key) and the probes are O(k).
Incorrect:
size_t probeBad(uint64_t h1, uint64_t h2, unsigned i, size_t m) {
  (void)h2; (void)i;
  return (size_t)(h1 % m);
}
Correct:
uint64_t bloomH1(const char *s) {
  uint64_t h = 5381;
  while (*s != '\0') h = h * 33 + (unsigned char)*s++;
  return h;
}
uint64_t bloomH2(const char *s) {
  uint64_t h = 14695981039346656037ULL;
  while (*s != '\0') { h ^= (unsigned char)*s++; h *= 1099511628211ULL; }
  return h | 1;
}
size_t bloomProbe(uint64_t h1, uint64_t h2, unsigned i, size_t m) {
  return (size_t)((h1 + (uint64_t)i * h2) % m);
}
Complexity: O(k)

