C · binary search trees · complexity · L2
Concept: Sorted input degenerates a plain BST into a list; build from the middle element to keep the height logarithmic
Misconception: A search tree always has O(log n) height.
Explanation: Operations cost O(height), and the height depends on the insertion order. Inserting 1, 2, 3, ... produces a chain of height n and O(n) operations. Building from a sorted array by taking the middle element as the root gives height about log2(n). Self-balancing trees (AVL, red-black) keep the height small automatically.
Incorrect:
typedef struct BNode { int key; struct BNode *left, *right; } BNode;
BNode *buildSequentialBad(const int *a, size_t n) {
  BNode *t = NULL;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) t = bstInsert(t, a[i]);
  return t;
}
Correct:
typedef struct BNode { int key; struct BNode *left, *right; } BNode;
BNode *bstFromSorted(const int *a, size_t lo, size_t hi) {
  if (lo >= hi) return NULL;
  size_t mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
  BNode *n = malloc(sizeof *n);
  if (n == NULL) return NULL;
  n->key = a[mid];
  n->left = bstFromSorted(a, lo, mid);
  n->right = bstFromSorted(a, mid + 1, hi);
  return n;
}
int bstHeight(const BNode *t) {
  if (t == NULL) return 0;
  int l = bstHeight(t->left), r = bstHeight(t->right);
  return 1 + (l > r ? l : r);
}
Complexity: O(n)

