C · B-trees and B+ trees · transformation · L3
Concept: Splitting a full node moves the median up to the parent and shrinks the original node to the keys below it
Misconception: A full child can be split by copying its upper half into a new node.
Explanation: With at most three keys per node, a full node is split around its median: the upper key moves to a new right sibling (with its children), the median is inserted into the parent between the two, and the old node keeps only the lower key. If the old node's count is not reduced, its upper keys appear twice. A split costs O(1) and is the only way the tree grows taller.
Incorrect:
typedef struct BT { int n, leaf; int keys[3]; struct BT *c[4]; } BT;
void btSplitBad(BT *p, int i, BT *y, BT *z) {
  z->leaf = y->leaf;
  z->n = 1;
  z->keys[0] = y->keys[2];
  if (!y->leaf) { z->c[0] = y->c[2]; z->c[1] = y->c[3]; }
  for (int j = p->n; j > i; j--) p->c[j + 1] = p->c[j];
  p->c[i + 1] = z;
  for (int j = p->n - 1; j >= i; j--) p->keys[j + 1] = p->keys[j];
  p->keys[i] = y->keys[1];
  p->n++;
}
Correct:
typedef struct BT { int n, leaf; int keys[3]; struct BT *c[4]; } BT;
void btSplit(BT *p, int i, BT *y, BT *z) {
  z->leaf = y->leaf;
  z->n = 1;
  z->keys[0] = y->keys[2];
  if (!y->leaf) { z->c[0] = y->c[2]; z->c[1] = y->c[3]; }
  y->n = 1;
  for (int j = p->n; j > i; j--) p->c[j + 1] = p->c[j];
  p->c[i + 1] = z;
  for (int j = p->n - 1; j >= i; j--) p->keys[j + 1] = p->keys[j];
  p->keys[i] = y->keys[1];
  p->n++;
}
Complexity: O(1)

