C · double-ended queues · access · L2
Concept: The back element is at (head + len - 1) % cap; without the modulo a wrapped deque reads past the array
Misconception: The last element is always at data[head + len - 1].
Explanation: After pushes at the front the used region can wrap around the end of the array. Both ends are then found with the modulo: front at head, back at (head + len - 1) % cap. Pop front advances head, pop back only shrinks len. Both are O(1) and both must refuse an empty deque.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow):
typedef struct { int *data; size_t head, len, cap; } Deque;
int dequePopBackBad(Deque *d) {
  int v = d->data[d->head + d->len - 1];
  d->len--;
  return v;
}
Correct:
typedef struct { int *data; size_t head, len, cap; } Deque;
int dequePopFront(Deque *d, int *out) {
  if (d->len == 0) return 0;
  *out = d->data[d->head];
  d->head = (d->head + 1) % d->cap;
  d->len--;
  return 1;
}
int dequePopBack(Deque *d, int *out) {
  if (d->len == 0) return 0;
  *out = d->data[(d->head + d->len - 1) % d->cap];
  d->len--;
  return 1;
}
Complexity: O(1)

