C · doubly linked lists · insertion-and-deletion · L2
Concept: Removing a node you hold a pointer to is O(1), but head and tail must be updated at the ends
Misconception: Unlinking the neighbors is all that removal needs.
Explanation: With prev and next the node can be unlinked without searching: join prev and next to each other. When the node is the head or the tail the list struct must change too; forgetting the tail leaves a dangling pointer that the next append writes through.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: heap-use-after-free):
typedef struct DNode { int val; struct DNode *prev, *next; } DNode;
typedef struct { DNode *head, *tail; size_t len; } DList;
void dlRemoveBad(DList *l, DNode *n) {
  if (n->prev != NULL) n->prev->next = n->next; else l->head = n->next;
  if (n->next != NULL) n->next->prev = n->prev;
  free(n);
  l->len--;
}
Correct:
typedef struct DNode { int val; struct DNode *prev, *next; } DNode;
typedef struct { DNode *head, *tail; size_t len; } DList;
void dlRemove(DList *l, DNode *n) {
  if (n->prev != NULL) n->prev->next = n->next; else l->head = n->next;
  if (n->next != NULL) n->next->prev = n->prev; else l->tail = n->prev;
  free(n);
  l->len--;
}
Complexity: O(1)

