C · dynamic arrays · transformation · L2
Concept: Remove duplicates from a sorted array in place with a read index and a write index
Misconception: The first element can always be kept, so start the write index at 1.
Explanation: In place deduplication walks a read index over the array and writes only values that differ from the last kept one. Start with the write index at 1 only after checking that the array is non-empty; for n = 0 the answer is 0, not 1. The result is O(n) time and O(1) space.
Incorrect:
size_t uniqueSortedBad(int *a, size_t n) {
  size_t w = 1;
  for (size_t i = 1; i < n; i++)
    if (a[i] != a[w - 1]) a[w++] = a[i];
  return w;
}
Correct:
size_t uniqueSorted(int *a, size_t n) {
  if (n == 0) return 0;
  size_t w = 1;
  for (size_t i = 1; i < n; i++)
    if (a[i] != a[w - 1]) a[w++] = a[i];
  return w;
}
Complexity: O(n)

