C · dynamic memory buffers · creation · L2
Concept: calloc zeroes the memory and checks the multiplication; with malloc you must do both yourself
Misconception: malloc(n * size) is the same as calloc(n, size).
Explanation: malloc leaves the memory uninitialized, and n * size can wrap around to a small number so the program gets a tiny block it believes is huge. calloc(n, size) returns zeroed memory, and a checked wrapper rejects counts whose product overflows before asking for memory.
Incorrect:
void *allocArrayBad(size_t n, size_t size) { return malloc(n * size); }
Correct:
void *allocArray(size_t n, size_t size) {
  if (size != 0 && n > SIZE_MAX / size) return NULL;
  return calloc(n, size);
}
Complexity: O(n)

