C · dynamic memory buffers · complexity · L3
Concept: Grow a buffer geometrically; adding a fixed amount makes n appends cost O(n^2)
Misconception: Growing the buffer by one slot per push is fine.
Explanation: Every growth may copy all elements. Growing by one copies on each push, O(n) per push and O(n^2) in total. Doubling the capacity copies fewer elements in total, so a push is O(1) amortized and the number of reallocations is about log2 n.
Incorrect:
typedef struct { int *data; size_t len, cap, grows; } IntBuf;
int bufPushBad(IntBuf *b, int v) {
  if (b->len == b->cap) {
    int *p = realloc(b->data, (b->cap + 1) * sizeof *p);
    if (p == NULL) return 0;
    b->data = p;
    b->cap++;
    b->grows++;
  }
  b->data[b->len++] = v;
  return 1;
}
Correct:
typedef struct { int *data; size_t len, cap, grows; } IntBuf;
int bufPush(IntBuf *b, int v) {
  if (b->len == b->cap) {
    size_t ncap = b->cap == 0 ? 4 : b->cap * 2;
    int *p = realloc(b->data, ncap * sizeof *p);
    if (p == NULL) return 0;
    b->data = p;
    b->cap = ncap;
    b->grows++;
  }
  b->data[b->len++] = v;
  return 1;
}
void bufFree(IntBuf *b) { free(b->data); b->data = NULL; b->len = b->cap = 0; }
Complexity: O(1) amortized

