C · dynamic memory buffers · creation · L1
Concept: malloc takes bytes: multiply the element count by the element size and check for overflow
Misconception: malloc(n) allocates room for n ints.
Explanation: malloc allocates a number of bytes. For an array of n ints ask for n * sizeof *a; writing malloc(n) gives a quarter of the space and the loop overruns it. Check that the multiplication cannot overflow, and check the result for NULL before using it.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow):
int *makeIotaBad(size_t n) {
  int *a = malloc(n);
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) a[i] = (int)i;
  return a;
}
Correct:
int *makeIota(size_t n) {
  if (n > SIZE_MAX / sizeof(int)) return NULL;
  int *a = malloc(n * sizeof *a);
  if (a == NULL) return NULL;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) a[i] = (int)i;
  return a;
}
Complexity: O(n)

