C · enum · edge-cases · L2
Concept: An enum variable can hold any int, so validate numbers that come from outside
Misconception: A variable of enum type can only hold one of its enumerators.
Explanation: In C an enum is an integer type, and a cast such as (enum Color)42 compiles and is not checked. Values read from files, the network or user input must be validated before use. Convert with a function that returns a status and writes the enum through a pointer only when the number is valid.
Incorrect:
enum Color { RED, GREEN, BLUE };
enum Color fromIntBad(int v) { return (enum Color)v; }
Correct:
enum Color { RED, GREEN, BLUE };
int toColor(int v, enum Color *out) {
  if (v < RED || v > BLUE) return 0;
  *out = (enum Color)v;
  return 1;
}
Complexity: O(1)

