C · Fenwick trees (binary indexed trees) · creation · L3
Concept: Build in O(n) by adding each node to its parent i + lowbit(i); copying the array is not a Fenwick tree
Misconception: A Fenwick tree can start as a copy of the input array.
Explanation: Each node must hold the sum of its lowbit(i) elements, so a plain copy is only right for odd indexes. The linear build copies the input into t[1..n] and then, for each i in increasing order, adds t[i] to its parent t[i + lowbit(i)] when that index is within n. That is O(n) instead of n updates at O(n log n).
Incorrect:
void fenBuildBad(long long *t, const int *a, size_t n) {
  for (size_t i = 1; i <= n; i++) t[i] = a[i - 1];
}
Correct:
int fenBuild(Fenwick *f, const int *a, size_t n) {
  if (!fenInit(f, n)) return 0;
  for (size_t i = 1; i <= n; i++) f->t[i] += a[i - 1];
  for (size_t i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    size_t j = i + lowbit(i);
    if (j <= n) f->t[j] += f->t[i];
  }
  return 1;
}
Complexity: O(n)

