C · function pointer · edge-cases · L1
Concept: Test an optional callback for NULL before calling it
Misconception: Calling a NULL function pointer does nothing.
Explanation: A NULL function pointer has no code to run, and calling it crashes the program. Optional callbacks should be tested before the call, with a documented default. Initialize unused slots in tables and structs to NULL on purpose so the check can find them.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: SEGV):
typedef int (*BinOp)(int, int);
int callNullBad(BinOp f, int a, int b) { return f(a, b); }
Correct:
typedef int (*BinOp)(int, int);
int callOr(BinOp f, int a, int b, int dflt) { return f != NULL ? f(a, b) : dflt; }
Complexity: O(1)

