C · binary heaps · complexity · L3
Concept: Build a heap in O(n) by sifting down from the last parent to the root
Misconception: Heapify can sift down the nodes in index order from the root.
Explanation: Sift-down assumes both subtrees are already heaps, so the nodes must be processed from the bottom: start at the last parent, index n / 2 - 1, and go down to 0. Most nodes are near the bottom and sink only a level or two, so the total work is O(n), better than n pushes at O(n log n). Going in index order breaks the assumption.
Incorrect:
void heapifyBad(int *a, size_t n) {
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n / 2; i++) {
    size_t j = i;
    for (;;) {
      size_t l = 2 * j + 1, r = l + 1, m = j;
      if (l < n && a[l] < a[m]) m = l;
      if (r < n && a[r] < a[m]) m = r;
      if (m == j) break;
      int t = a[m]; a[m] = a[j]; a[j] = t;
      j = m;
    }
  }
}
Correct:
void hSiftDown(int *a, size_t n, size_t i) {
  for (;;) {
    size_t l = 2 * i + 1, r = l + 1, m = i;
    if (l < n && a[l] < a[m]) m = l;
    if (r < n && a[r] < a[m]) m = r;
    if (m == i) break;
    int t = a[m]; a[m] = a[i]; a[i] = t;
    i = m;
  }
}
void heapify(int *a, size_t n) {
  for (size_t i = n / 2; i-- > 0;) hSiftDown(a, n, i);
}
Complexity: O(n)

