C · binary heaps · insertion-and-deletion · L2
Concept: Pop moves the last element to the root and sifts down toward the smaller child
Misconception: Sift-down can swap with the left child whenever it is smaller than the node.
Explanation: After the root is removed, the last element takes its place and sinks. At each step it must swap with the smaller of the two children, otherwise the larger child moves above the smaller one and the heap property is violated. Checking only the left child leaves the right subtree unordered. Pop is O(log n).
Incorrect:
void popBad(int *a, size_t *n, int *out) {
  *out = a[0];
  a[0] = a[--(*n)];
  size_t i = 0;
  for (;;) {
    size_t l = 2 * i + 1;
    if (l >= *n || a[l] >= a[i]) break;
    int t = a[l]; a[l] = a[i]; a[i] = t;
    i = l;
  }
}
Correct:
typedef struct { int *a; size_t n, cap; } MinHeap;
void hSiftDown(int *a, size_t n, size_t i) {
  for (;;) {
    size_t l = 2 * i + 1, r = l + 1, m = i;
    if (l < n && a[l] < a[m]) m = l;
    if (r < n && a[r] < a[m]) m = r;
    if (m == i) break;
    int t = a[m]; a[m] = a[i]; a[i] = t;
    i = m;
  }
}
int heapPop(MinHeap *h, int *out) {
  if (h->n == 0) return 0;
  *out = h->a[0];
  h->a[0] = h->a[--h->n];
  hSiftDown(h->a, h->n, 0);
  return 1;
}
Complexity: O(log n)

