C · intbits · transformation · L3
Concept: Serialize integers with shifts; copying the in-memory bytes depends on the machine byte order
Misconception: memcpy of a uint32_t gives the bytes in network order.
Explanation: On little-endian machines (x86, ARM) the least significant byte is stored first, so memcpy writes 04 03 02 01 for 0x01020304. A file or network format needs a fixed order. Extract each byte with shifts, which does not depend on how the machine stores the value.
Incorrect:
void packBad(uint32_t x, uint8_t out[4]) {
  memcpy(out, &x, 4);
}
Correct:
void packBE(uint32_t x, uint8_t out[4]) {
  out[0] = (uint8_t)(x >> 24);
  out[1] = (uint8_t)(x >> 16);
  out[2] = (uint8_t)(x >> 8);
  out[3] = (uint8_t)x;
}
Complexity: O(1)

