C · intbits · patterns · L2
Concept: Test a power of two with parentheses: == binds tighter than &
Misconception: x & (x - 1) == 0 tests whether x is a power of two.
Explanation: == has higher precedence than &, so the expression means x & ((x - 1) == 0). The compiler warns with -Wparentheses. Write (x & (x - 1)) == 0, and exclude zero: x != 0 && (x & (x - 1)) == 0.
Incorrect (compiles with a warning: parentheses):
bool pow2Bad(uint32_t x) {
  return x & (x - 1) == 0;
}
Correct:
bool isPow2(uint32_t x) {
  return x != 0 && (x & (x - 1)) == 0;
}
Complexity: O(1)

