C · intbits · transformation · L3
Concept: 1 << 31 overflows a signed int; use an unsigned constant for bit masks
Misconception: 1 << 31 is the mask for the top bit of a 32-bit value.
Explanation: The literal 1 has type int, and shifting a 1 into the sign bit is undefined behaviour (UBSan: left shift cannot be represented). Use an unsigned constant, 1u << 31 or UINT32_C(1) << 31. A shift by the width of the type or more is also undefined.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: left shift):
uint32_t topBitBad(volatile int n) {
  return (uint32_t)(1 << n);
}
Correct:
uint32_t topBit(unsigned n) {
  return UINT32_C(1) << n;
}
Complexity: O(1)

