C · pointer · mutation · L3
Concept: To change the caller's pointer, pass a pointer to it
Misconception: Assigning to a pointer parameter changes the caller's pointer.
Explanation: C passes the pointer by value, so assigning to the parameter changes only the copy. To free-and-null or to allocate on behalf of the caller, take an int ** and assign through it with *pp. The same pattern is used to move a list head or to return a new object through an out-parameter.
Incorrect:
void clearBad(int *p) {
  free(p);
  p = NULL;
}
Correct:
void releaseInt(int **pp) {
  free(*pp);
  *pp = NULL;
}
Complexity: O(1)

