C · pointer · insertion-and-deletion · L2
Concept: Reading memory after free is undefined; copy what you need first and null the pointer
Misconception: Memory still holds its value right after free.
Explanation: free returns the block to the allocator, and any later read or write is a use-after-free. It may appear to work until the block is reused. Read the value you need before freeing, then set the pointer to NULL so a later mistake fails loudly instead of corrupting memory.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: heap-use-after-free):
int useAfterFreeBad(void) {
  int *p = malloc(sizeof *p);
  *p = 7;
  free(p);
  return *p;
}
Correct:
int useThenFree(void) {
  int *p = malloc(sizeof *p);
  if (p == NULL) return -1;
  *p = 7;
  int v = *p;
  free(p);
  p = NULL;
  return v;
}
Complexity: O(1)

