C · priority queues · patterns · L4
Concept: Dijkstra pops the smallest tentative distance and skips stale entries; BFS hop counts are wrong for weighted edges
Misconception: Breadth-first search finds shortest paths in a weighted graph.
Explanation: BFS minimises the number of edges, not the total weight. Dijkstra keeps a min-priority queue of (distance, node), pops the smallest, ignores entries whose distance is larger than the best known one (lazy deletion), and relaxes the outgoing edges. It needs non-negative weights and runs in O((V + E) log V) with an adjacency list; with a matrix, as here, scanning a row costs O(V).
Incorrect:
void bfsHops(const int w[5][5], int src, long long dist[5]) {
  int queue[5], head = 0, tail = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) dist[i] = -1;
  dist[src] = 0;
  queue[tail++] = src;
  while (head < tail) {
    int u = queue[head++];
    for (int v = 0; v < 5; v++)
      if (w[u][v] > 0 && dist[v] < 0) { dist[v] = dist[u] + 1; queue[tail++] = v; }
  }
}
Correct:
void dijkstra(const int w[5][5], int src, long long dist[5]) {
  for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) dist[i] = LLONG_MAX;
  dist[src] = 0;
  PQ q;
  pqInit(&q, itemLess);
  pqPush(&q, 0, src);
  Item it;
  while (pqPop(&q, &it)) {
    if (it.key > dist[it.id]) continue;
    for (int v = 0; v < 5; v++) {
      if (w[it.id][v] <= 0) continue;
      long long nd = it.key + w[it.id][v];
      if (nd < dist[v]) { dist[v] = nd; pqPush(&q, nd, v); }
    }
  }
  pqFree(&q);
}
Complexity: O(V^2 log V)

