C · priority queues · patterns · L4
Concept: Merge k sorted arrays with a heap of size k; after each pop push the next element of the same array
Misconception: The merge only needs the first element of every array.
Explanation: The smallest remaining value is always at the head of one of the k arrays. Keep a heap with one head per array, pop the minimum, append it to the output and push the next element of the array it came from. Skipping that refill loses every value after the first of each array. The cost is O(N log k) for N total values.
Incorrect:
size_t mergeKBad(const int *const arrs[], const size_t lens[], size_t k, int *out) {
  size_t n = 0;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < k; i++)
    if (lens[i] > 0) out[n++] = arrs[i][0];
  return n;
}
Correct:
size_t mergeK(const int *const arrs[], const size_t lens[], size_t k, int *out) {
  size_t pos[8], total = 0;
  if (k > 8) return 0;
  PQ q;
  pqInit(&q, itemLess);
  for (size_t i = 0; i < k; i++) {
    pos[i] = 0;
    if (lens[i] > 0) { pqPush(&q, arrs[i][0], (int)i); pos[i] = 1; }
  }
  Item it;
  while (pqPop(&q, &it)) {
    out[total++] = (int)it.key;
    size_t i = (size_t)it.id;
    if (pos[i] < lens[i]) pqPush(&q, arrs[i][pos[i]++], it.id);
  }
  pqFree(&q);
  return total;
}
Complexity: O(N log k)

