C · red-black trees · complexity · L2
Concept: The height of a red-black tree is at most 2 log2(n + 1), so operations are O(log n) even for sorted input
Misconception: Insertion order does not matter much for a binary search tree.
Explanation: In a plain binary search tree sorted input builds a chain of height n. The red-black rules guarantee that the longest path is at most twice the shortest one, giving height at most 2 log2(n + 1). Inserting a thousand sorted keys yields a height of about 15 instead of 1000.
Incorrect:
typedef struct RNode { int key, red; struct RNode *l, *r; } RNode;
int isRed(const RNode *n) { return n != NULL && n->red; }
RNode *plainInsertBad(RNode *t, int key) {
  if (t == NULL) return rbNew(key);
  if (key < t->key) t->l = plainInsertBad(t->l, key);
  else if (key > t->key) t->r = plainInsertBad(t->r, key);
  return t;
}
Correct:
typedef struct RNode { int key, red; struct RNode *l, *r; } RNode;
int isRed(const RNode *n) { return n != NULL && n->red; }
int rbHeight(const RNode *t) {
  if (t == NULL) return 0;
  int l = rbHeight(t->l), r = rbHeight(t->r);
  return 1 + (l > r ? l : r);
}
Complexity: O(log n)

