C · segment trees · mutation · L3
Concept: A point update must recompute every ancestor on the way back up
Misconception: Changing the leaf is enough, because queries read the leaves.
Explanation: Queries use the stored value of any node that is fully covered, so ancestors hold copies of the sums. A point update goes down to the leaf, sets it, and on the way back recomputes each node from its two children. Updating only the leaf leaves stale sums above it. The cost is O(log n).
Incorrect:
static void urecBad(long long *t, size_t node, size_t lo, size_t hi, size_t i, int v) {
  if (lo == hi) { t[node] = v; return; }
  size_t mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
  if (i <= mid) urecBad(t, 2 * node + 1, lo, mid, i, v);
  else urecBad(t, 2 * node + 2, mid + 1, hi, i, v);
}
void segSetBad(long long *t, size_t n, size_t i, int v) { urecBad(t, 0, 0, n - 1, i, v); }
Correct:
static void urec(long long *t, size_t node, size_t lo, size_t hi, size_t i, int v) {
  if (lo == hi) { t[node] = v; return; }
  size_t mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
  if (i <= mid) urec(t, 2 * node + 1, lo, mid, i, v);
  else urec(t, 2 * node + 2, mid + 1, hi, i, v);
  t[node] = t[2 * node + 1] + t[2 * node + 2];
}
int segSet(SegTree *s, size_t i, int v) {
  if (i >= s->n) return 0;
  urec(s->t, 0, 0, s->n - 1, i, v);
  return 1;
}
Complexity: O(log n)

