C · singly linked list · patterns · L3
Concept: Detect a cycle with a slow and a fast pointer; checking only for a node that points to itself misses most cycles
Misconception: A cycle exists only if a node points to itself.
Explanation: A cycle can rejoin the list at any node. Floyd's algorithm advances slow by one and fast by two; if they ever meet there is a cycle, and if fast reaches NULL there is none. It needs O(n) time and O(1) memory.
Incorrect:
typedef struct Node { int val; struct Node *next; } Node;
int hasCycleBad(const Node *head) {
  const Node *p = head;
  for (int i = 0; i < 100 && p != NULL; i++, p = p->next)
    if (p->next == p) return 1;
  return 0;
}
Correct:
typedef struct Node { int val; struct Node *next; } Node;
int hasCycle(const Node *head) {
  const Node *slow = head, *fast = head;
  while (fast != NULL && fast->next != NULL) {
    slow = slow->next;
    fast = fast->next->next;
    if (slow == fast) return 1;
  }
  return 0;
}
Complexity: O(n)

