document:
C · singly linked list · insertion-and-deletion · L1
Concept: Inserting at the head is O(1): point the new node at the old head and make it the head
Misconception: Inserting at the head loses the rest of the list unless you copy it.
Explanation: A new node takes the old head as its next pointer and becomes the head, so nothing moves and the cost is constant. The function must change the caller's head, so it takes a Node ** (or returns the new head). Forgetting to link the old head drops the whole list.
Incorrect:
typedef struct Node { int val; struct Node *next; } Node;
Node *pushFrontBad(Node *head, int v) {
  Node *n = malloc(sizeof *n);
  if (n == NULL) return head;
  n->val = v;
  n->next = NULL;
  return n;
}
Correct:
typedef struct Node { int val; struct Node *next; } Node;
int pushFront(Node **head, int v) {
  Node *n = malloc(sizeof *n);
  if (n == NULL) return 0;
  n->val = v;
  n->next = *head;
  *head = n;
  return 1;
}
void listFree(Node *h) {
  while (h != NULL) {
    Node *next = h->next;
    free(h);
    h = next;
  }
}
Complexity: O(1)

