C · singly linked list · transformation · L2
Concept: Reverse in place with three pointers: save next before you overwrite it
Misconception: Setting node->next = prev is all a reversal needs.
Explanation: Overwriting next loses the rest of the list unless it was saved first. Keep prev, cur and a saved next: save cur->next, point cur at prev, advance both. The reversal is O(n) time and O(1) extra space, and the old head becomes the tail.
Incorrect:
typedef struct Node { int val; struct Node *next; } Node;
Node *reverseBad(Node *head) {
  Node *prev = NULL;
  while (head != NULL) {
    head->next = prev;
    prev = head;
    head = head->next;
  }
  return prev;
}
Correct:
typedef struct Node { int val; struct Node *next; } Node;
Node *reverseList(Node *head) {
  Node *prev = NULL;
  while (head != NULL) {
    Node *next = head->next;
    head->next = prev;
    prev = head;
    head = next;
  }
  return prev;
}
Complexity: O(n)

