C · stacks · insertion-and-deletion · L1
Concept: Check capacity before every push and grow the array; an unchecked push writes past the buffer
Misconception: A stack backed by an array always has room for the next element.
Explanation: An array stack stores elements in data[0..len) and pushes at data[len]. When len equals cap the next write is one past the allocation, a heap buffer overflow. Grow geometrically with realloc through a temporary so pushes stay O(1) amortized and a failed realloc does not lose the buffer.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow):
typedef struct { int *data; size_t len, cap; } Stack;
int stackInitBad(Stack *s, size_t cap) {
  s->data = malloc(cap * sizeof *s->data);
  s->len = 0;
  s->cap = cap;
  return s->data != NULL;
}
void stackPushBad(Stack *s, int v) { s->data[s->len++] = v; }
Correct:
typedef struct { int *data; size_t len, cap; } Stack;
int stackInit(Stack *s, size_t cap) {
  s->data = malloc(cap * sizeof *s->data);
  s->len = 0;
  s->cap = s->data != NULL ? cap : 0;
  return s->data != NULL;
}
int stackPush(Stack *s, int v) {
  if (s->len == s->cap) {
    size_t ncap = s->cap != 0 ? s->cap * 2 : 4;
    int *p = realloc(s->data, ncap * sizeof *p);
    if (p == NULL) return 0;
    s->data = p;
    s->cap = ncap;
  }
  s->data[s->len++] = v;
  return 1;
}
void stackFree(Stack *s) {
  free(s->data);
  s->data = NULL;
  s->len = s->cap = 0;
}
Complexity: O(1) amortized

