C · string · mutation · L2
Concept: A string literal is read-only; copy it into an array before changing it
Misconception: char *s = "abc" gives a string you can modify.
Explanation: A literal lives in read-only memory, so writing through a char * that points to it is undefined and usually crashes with a segmentation fault. char s[] = "abc" makes a writable copy on the stack. Declare literal pointers as const char * so the compiler can catch writes.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: SEGV):
void upperLiteralBad(void) {
  char *s = "abc";
  s[0] = 'A';
}
Correct:
void upperInPlace(char *s) {
  for (; *s != '\0'; s++) *s = (char)toupper((unsigned char)*s);
}
Complexity: O(n)

