C · string · mutation · L1
Concept: strcpy has no size limit; copy with a capacity and truncate
Misconception: strcpy into a small buffer is safe if the string looks short.
Explanation: strcpy copies until the terminating zero and never checks the destination size, so a longer source overwrites the memory after the buffer. Pass the capacity and use snprintf, which always terminates and returns the length it wanted to write, so the caller can detect truncation.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: stack-buffer-overflow):
void setBad(char *dst, const char *s) { strcpy(dst, s); }
Correct:
size_t setGood(char *dst, size_t cap, const char *s) {
  int n = snprintf(dst, cap, "%s", s);
  return n < 0 ? 0 : (size_t)n;
}
Complexity: O(n)

