document:
C · string · edge-cases · L2
Concept: strncpy does not always write a terminator; add one yourself
Misconception: strncpy(dst, s, n) always leaves a valid string.
Explanation: When the source has n or more chars, strncpy copies n chars and writes no terminator, so the result is not a string. It also pads shorter copies with zeros up to n, which is wasted work. Terminate explicitly at dst[cap - 1], or use snprintf.
Incorrect:
void copyBad(char *dst, size_t cap, const char *s) { strncpy(dst, s, cap); }
Correct:
void copyGood(char *dst, size_t cap, const char *s) {
  if (cap == 0) return;
  strncpy(dst, s, cap - 1);
  dst[cap - 1] = '\0';
}
Complexity: O(n)

