C · struct · mutation · L1
Concept: A struct passed by value is a copy; changes need a pointer
Misconception: A function that takes a struct can change the caller's struct.
Explanation: C passes struct arguments by value: the callee works on a copy and the caller's struct stays unchanged. Pass a pointer (struct P *p) and use p->x to modify the original. Pass const struct P * to read a large struct without copying it.
Incorrect:
struct P { int x; int y; };
void moveBad(struct P p) { p.x += 1; }
Correct:
struct P { int x; int y; };
void moveRight(struct P *p) { p->x += 1; }
Complexity: O(1)

