C · struct · access · L2
Concept: Use offsetof for member offsets; adding up sizeof of earlier members ignores padding
Misconception: The offset of a member is the sum of the sizes of the members before it.
Explanation: Padding sits between members, so the real offset of b in struct { char a; int b; } is 4, not 1. offsetof(type, member) from <stddef.h> gives the true byte offset and is what serializers, intrusive lists and container_of tricks should use.
Incorrect:
struct Rec { char a; int b; short c; };
size_t offBad(void) { return sizeof(char); }
Correct:
struct Rec { char a; int b; short c; };
size_t offGood(void) { return offsetof(struct Rec, b); }
Complexity: O(1)

