C · struct · creation · L2
Concept: A struct cannot contain itself; a self-referential node holds a pointer to its own type
Misconception: struct Node { int v; struct Node next; } builds a linked node.
Explanation: A struct that contained itself would have infinite size, so the compiler reports an incomplete type. A node holds a pointer to the next node instead, because a pointer has a fixed size even while the pointed-to type is still being defined.
Incorrect (does not compile: incomplete type):
struct NodeBad {
  int v;
  struct NodeBad next;
};
Correct:
struct Node {
  int v;
  struct Node *next;
};
Complexity: O(1)

