Concept: Set tm_isdst to -1 when building local time so mktime decides whether daylight saving applies
Misconception: tm_isdst = 0 is a safe default for mktime.
Explanation: tm_isdst of 0 tells mktime that daylight saving time is not in effect, so a July date in New York is read as standard time and ends up one hour off. Zero-initialize the struct, fill the fields and set tm_isdst to -1 to let the library consult the time zone rules.
Incorrect:
time_t makeLocalBad(int y, int mo, int d, int h) {
  struct tm tm = { 0 };
  tm.tm_year = y - 1900; tm.tm_mon = mo - 1; tm.tm_mday = d; tm.tm_hour = h;
  tm.tm_isdst = 0;
  return mktime(&tm);
}
Correct:
time_t makeLocal(int y, int mo, int d, int h) {
  struct tm tm = { 0 };
  tm.tm_year = y - 1900; tm.tm_mon = mo - 1; tm.tm_mday = d; tm.tm_hour = h;
  tm.tm_isdst = -1;
  return mktime(&tm);
}
Complexity: O(1)

