C · typedef · edge-cases · L2
Concept: size_t is unsigned, so a loop counting down to 0 with i >= 0 never ends
Misconception: for (size_t i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) visits the elements backwards.
Explanation: size_t is an unsigned type used for sizes and indexes, so i >= 0 is always true and i-- wraps from 0 to the maximum value. With n == 0 even the start n - 1 wraps. The idiom for i from n - 1 down to 0 is for (size_t i = n; i-- > 0;). gcc warns with -Wtype-limits.
Incorrect (compiles with a warning: always true):
int lastNegativeBad(const int *a, size_t n) {
  for (size_t i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--)
    if (a[i] < 0) return (int)i;
  return -1;
}
Correct:
int lastNegative(const int *a, size_t n) {
  for (size_t i = n; i-- > 0;)
    if (a[i] < 0) return (int)i;
  return -1;
}
Complexity: O(n)

