C · typedef · creation · L1
Concept: typedef struct gives the type a short name; without it every use needs the struct keyword
Misconception: After struct Point { ... }; you can write Point p; in C.
Explanation: In C a struct tag lives in its own namespace, so the type is struct Point, not Point. A typedef adds a plain type name: typedef struct Point { int x; int y; } Point;. Using the bare name without the typedef is a compile error (unknown type name).
Incorrect (does not compile: unknown type name):
struct Point { int x; int y; };
Point makePointBad(int x, int y) {
  Point p = { x, y };
  return p;
}
Correct:
typedef struct Point { int x; int y; } Point;
Point makePoint(int x, int y) {
  Point p = { x, y };
  return p;
}
Complexity: O(1)

