C · union · creation · L2
Concept: A plain initializer list initializes the first member only; use a designated initializer for another one
Misconception: union U u = {2.5} stores 2.5 in the double member.
Explanation: A brace initializer for a union applies to the first member. With union { int i; double d; } the value 2.5 is converted to int and stored in i as 2. A designated initializer such as { .d = 2.5 } selects the member explicitly.
Incorrect:
union Num { int i; double d; };
int initBad(void) {
  union Num u = { 2.5 };
  return u.i;
}
Correct:
union Num { int i; double d; };
double initGood(void) {
  union Num u = { .d = 2.5 };
  return u.d;
}
Complexity: O(1)

