C · union · mental-model · L1
Concept: A union is as large as its largest member, not the sum of its members
Misconception: A union with an int and a double needs 12 bytes.
Explanation: All members of a union start at the same address and overlap, so only one holds a value at a time. The size is that of the largest member, rounded up to the alignment of the strictest member: union { int i; double d; } is 8 bytes on common 64-bit targets.
Incorrect:
union SizeBad { int i; double d; };
size_t unionBadSize(void) { return sizeof(int) + sizeof(double); }
Correct:
union SizeGood { int i; double d; };
size_t unionSize(void) { return sizeof(union SizeGood); }
Complexity: O(1)

