C · union · access · L2
Concept: A union does not remember its active member, so store a tag next to it
Misconception: Reading a union member tells you what it holds.
Explanation: Reading a member other than the one last written reinterprets the bytes. The union has no record of which member is active. Wrap it in a struct with an enum tag, set the tag whenever you write a member, and switch on the tag before reading.
Incorrect:
union Raw { int i; double d; };
int asIntBad(double x) {
  union Raw r;
  r.d = x;
  return r.i;
}
Correct:
enum Kind { K_INT, K_DOUBLE };
struct Value {
  enum Kind kind;
  union { int i; double d; } as;
};
struct Value fromDouble(double x) {
  struct Value v = { K_DOUBLE, { .d = x } };
  return v;
}
int valueToInt(struct Value v) {
  return v.kind == K_INT ? v.as.i : (int)v.as.d;
}
Complexity: O(1)

