C · void pointer · equality · L2
Concept: Compare generic elements with memcmp over the element size, not one byte
Misconception: Comparing the first byte of each element finds a match.
Explanation: Elements of unknown type are equal when all of their bytes are equal. Comparing only the first byte gives false matches, for example 256 and 0 on a little-endian machine. Use memcmp(elem, key, size) per element. memcmp is not suitable for types with padding or floating-point values, where a comparator function is needed.
Incorrect:
long findBad(const void *base, size_t n, size_t size, const void *key) {
  const unsigned char *p = base;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
    if (p[i * size] == *(const unsigned char *)key) return (long)i;
  return -1;
}
Correct:
long findBytes(const void *base, size_t n, size_t size, const void *key) {
  const unsigned char *p = base;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
    if (memcmp(p + i * size, key, size) == 0) return (long)i;
  return -1;
}
Complexity: O(n * size)

