C · void pointer · mutation · L2
Concept: A generic swap needs the element size; copy that many bytes through a temporary
Misconception: Swapping through void * can use an int temporary.
Explanation: A generic function cannot know the element type, so it takes a size and moves bytes. A fixed int temporary copies only four bytes of an eight-byte double and leaves the rest unchanged. Swap byte by byte, or with memcpy through a buffer, for exactly size bytes.
Incorrect:
void swapBad(void *a, void *b) {
  int t = *(int *)a;
  *(int *)a = *(int *)b;
  *(int *)b = t;
}
Correct:
void swapBytes(void *a, void *b, size_t size) {
  unsigned char *x = a, *y = b;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < size; i++) {
    unsigned char t = x[i];
    x[i] = y[i];
    y[i] = t;
  }
}
Complexity: O(size)

