Concept: Make a pin an output with DDRx |= (1 << bit) so the other pins keep their direction
Misconception: Writing DDRB = (1 << PB5) just turns on pin PB5.
Explanation: DDRx has one bit per pin: 1 is output, 0 is input. A plain assignment writes all eight bits, so every other pin of the port silently goes back to input. Use read-modify-write with OR to touch only the named bit. The bit name PB5 is just the number 5, so (1 << PB5) is the mask 0x20.
Incorrect:
void ledInitBad(void) {
  DDRB = (1 << PB5);
}
Correct:
void ledInit(void) {
  DDRB |= (1 << PB5);
}
Complexity: O(1)

