Context: platform: AVR_Bare_Metal
Embedded C · AVR_Bare_Metal · avr gpio registers · mutation · Intermediate
Concept: Toggle one output with XOR, not by inverting the whole port register
Misconception: PORTB = ~PORTB toggles the LED.
Explanation: Inverting the whole register flips all eight pins, including pins that drive other loads and pins whose PORT bit enables a pull-up. XOR with a one-bit mask flips only that bit. On the ATmega328P the datasheet also lets you toggle by writing a 1 to the PINx bit, which does the same in a single instruction; the XOR form works on every AVR.
Incorrect:
void ledToggleBad(void) {
  PORTB = ~PORTB;
}
Correct:
void ledToggle(void) {
  PORTB ^= (1 << PB5);
}
Complexity: O(1)

