Context: platform: AVR_Bare_Metal
Embedded C · AVR_Bare_Metal · avr timer1 and timer0 · fit-and-alternatives · Intermediate
Concept: Pick the smallest prescaler whose compare value still fits in 16 bits
Misconception: Always using the largest prescaler (1024) is the safe choice.
Explanation: A smaller prescaler gives finer resolution and less jitter, but OCR1A is only 16 bits wide (at most 65535). Try the prescalers 1, 8, 64, 256 and 1024 in order and take the first whose compare value fits. A very slow tick with a small prescaler would overflow the 16-bit register and wrap silently.
Incorrect:
uint16_t prescBad(uint32_t freq) {
  (void)freq;
  return 1024;
}
Correct:
uint16_t timer1Prescaler(uint32_t freq) {
  static const uint16_t opts[5] = {1, 8, 64, 256, 1024};
  for (uint8_t i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    uint32_t ticks = F_CPU / ((uint32_t)opts[i] * freq);
    if (ticks >= 1 && ticks - 1 <= 65535UL) return opts[i];
  }
  return 0;
}
Complexity: O(1)

