Context: platform: STM32_HAL
Embedded C · STM32_HAL · bitwise operations · transformation · Intermediate
Concept: Reverse the bits of a byte by swapping halves, pairs and single bits
Misconception: Swapping the two nibbles reverses a byte.
Explanation: Reversing the bit order means bit 0 swaps with bit 7, bit 1 with bit 6, and so on. Three masked swaps do it: nibbles, then pairs inside each nibble, then single bits. Swapping only the nibbles moves 0x01 to 0x10 instead of 0x80. Bit reversal is needed when a device sends the least significant bit first.
Incorrect:
uint8_t revBad(uint8_t b) {
  return (uint8_t)((b >> 4) | (b << 4));
}
Correct:
uint8_t bitRev8(uint8_t b) {
  b = (uint8_t)(((b & 0xF0U) >> 4) | ((b & 0x0FU) << 4));
  b = (uint8_t)(((b & 0xCCU) >> 2) | ((b & 0x33U) << 2));
  b = (uint8_t)(((b & 0xAAU) >> 1) | ((b & 0x55U) << 1));
  return b;
}
Complexity: O(1)

