Context: platform: STM32_HAL
Embedded C · STM32_HAL · volatile and race conditions · iteration · Advanced
Concept: Read a 64-bit counter updated by an interrupt by reading the high word before and after the low word
Misconception: A 64-bit counter can be read as two 32-bit loads at any time.
Explanation: A Cortex-M core loads 32 bits at a time, so reading a 64-bit tick counter takes two loads, and an interrupt between them can carry from the low word into the high word. The result is a value that never existed, off by 2^32. Read the high word, then the low word, then the high word again, and retry when the two high words differ; no interrupt masking is needed.
Incorrect:
uint64_t readBad(volatile uint32_t *lo, volatile uint32_t *hi, void (*between)(void)) {
  uint32_t l = *lo;
  between();
  uint32_t h = *hi;
  return ((uint64_t)h << 32) | l;
}
Correct:
uint64_t read64(volatile uint32_t *lo, volatile uint32_t *hi, void (*between)(void)) {
  uint32_t h1, l, h2;
  do {
    h1 = *hi; between();
    l = *lo; between();
    h2 = *hi;
  } while (h1 != h2);
  return ((uint64_t)h2 << 32) | l;
}
Complexity: O(1)

