Context: platform: STM32_HAL
Embedded C · STM32_HAL · volatile and race conditions · mental-model · Beginner
Concept: Mark a variable shared with an interrupt volatile so each read really happens
Misconception: A variable that an interrupt handler sets can be read normally in the main loop.
Explanation: The compiler optimises the main loop as if nothing else could change a variable. A flag set by an interrupt must be volatile, otherwise the compiler may read it once and keep the value in a register, and the loop never ends. In the compiled Cortex-M code the difference shows as a second load instruction. volatile does not make a multi-step operation atomic; it only keeps each access.
Incorrect:
int doneB;
int pollBad(void) {
  int a = doneB;
  int b = doneB;
  return a + b;
}
Correct:
static volatile int done;
int poll(void) {
  int a = done;
  int b = done;
  return a + b;
}
void setDone(int v) { done = v; }
Complexity: O(1)

