Context: platform: STM32_HAL
Embedded C · STM32_HAL · crc calculation · patterns · Intermediate
Concept: Replace the inner bit loop with a 256-entry lookup table built once
Misconception: A table-driven CRC needs a different table for every message length.
Explanation: The table holds the result of 8 shift steps for every possible byte, so one table lookup replaces the bit loop. It does not depend on the message length. Build it once at start-up (or at compile time and store it in flash) and use crc = (crc >> 8) ^ table[(crc ^ byte) & 0xFF] for the reflected CRC-32. Check that table[1] is 0x77073096 and that the result matches the bitwise version.
Incorrect:
uint32_t tabBad[256];
void tabBadInit(void) {
  for (uint32_t i = 0; i < 256; i++) tabBad[i] = i;
}
Correct:
static uint32_t crcTab[256];
void crcTabInit(void) {
  for (uint32_t i = 0; i < 256; i++) {
    uint32_t c = i;
    for (int k = 0; k < 8; k++) c = (c & 1U) ? (c >> 1) ^ 0xEDB88320U : c >> 1;
    crcTab[i] = c;
  }
}
uint32_t crc32Tab(const uint8_t *p, size_t n) {
  uint32_t crc = 0xFFFFFFFFU;
  while (n--) crc = (crc >> 8) ^ crcTab[(crc ^ *p++) & 0xFFU];
  return crc ^ 0xFFFFFFFFU;
}
Complexity: O(n)

