Context: platform: STM32_HAL
Embedded C · STM32_HAL · checksum · mental-model · Beginner
Concept: Compute an NMEA 0183 checksum as the XOR of the characters between the dollar sign and the asterisk
Misconception: The NMEA checksum covers every character of the sentence.
Explanation: An NMEA sentence looks like $GPGLL,...*31. The two hex digits after the asterisk are the XOR of all characters between the $ and the *, exclusive of both. XOR is cheap and catches any single-bit error, but it cannot detect two errors in the same bit position and it ignores byte order. Include the $ in the loop and every checksum is wrong.
Incorrect:
uint8_t nmeaBad(const char *s) {
  uint8_t x = 0;
  while (*s && *s != '*') x ^= (uint8_t)*s++;
  return x;
}
Correct:
uint8_t nmeaCsum(const char *s) {
  uint8_t x = 0;
  if (*s == '$') s++;
  while (*s && *s != '*') x ^= (uint8_t)*s++;
  return x;
}
Complexity: O(n)

