Embedded C · STM32_HAL · i2c master · patterns · Intermediate
Concept: Read a sensor register and report failure instead of returning stale data
Misconception: A register read always produces a valid byte.
Explanation: HAL_I2C_Mem_Read returns a status. When the device does not acknowledge, the buffer is left as it was, so a function that ignores the status hands the caller whatever the buffer held, which looks like a real reading. Check the status, write the result through an output pointer only on success, and return an error code the caller can test.
Incorrect:
uint8_t regReadBad(I2C_HandleTypeDef *h, uint8_t addr7, uint8_t reg) {
  uint8_t v = 0xFF;
  HAL_I2C_Mem_Read(h, i2cDev(addr7), reg, I2C_MEMADD_SIZE_8BIT, &v, 1, 10);
  return v;
}
Correct:
int i2cRegRead(I2C_HandleTypeDef *h, uint8_t addr7, uint8_t reg, uint8_t *out) {
  uint8_t v;
  if (HAL_I2C_Mem_Read(h, i2cDev(addr7), reg, I2C_MEMADD_SIZE_8BIT, &v, 1, 10) != HAL_OK)
    return -1;
  *out = v;
  return 0;
}
Complexity: O(1)

