Context: platform: AVR_Bare_Metal
Embedded C · AVR_Bare_Metal · non-blocking isr design · patterns · Intermediate
Concept: Find which pins caused a pin-change interrupt by XOR-ing the new port value with the old one
Misconception: The pin-change vector tells you which pin changed.
Explanation: One pin-change interrupt vector serves a whole group of pins (eight on PORTB of the ATmega328P) and does not say which one changed. Keep the previous port value in a variable, XOR it with the current value to get the changed bits, and AND the changed bits with the current value to get the rising ones; changed AND NOT current gives the falling ones.
Incorrect:
uint8_t changedBad(uint8_t now) {
  return now;
}
Correct:
static uint8_t lastPins;
void pinChanges(uint8_t now, uint8_t *rising, uint8_t *falling) {
  uint8_t changed = lastPins ^ now;
  *rising = changed & now;
  *falling = changed & (uint8_t)~now;
  lastPins = now;
}
Complexity: O(1)

