Context: platform: AVR_Bare_Metal
Embedded C · AVR_Bare_Metal · non-blocking isr design · creation · Intermediate
Concept: Pass bytes from an ISR to main code with a power-of-two ring buffer that keeps one slot free
Misconception: A ring buffer of 8 slots can hold 8 bytes.
Explanation: With only a head and a tail index, head == tail must mean empty, so full has to be detected earlier: the buffer is full when advancing head would make it equal to tail. That wastes one slot, so 8 slots hold 7 bytes. With a power-of-two size the wrap is a mask instead of a division, which is cheap on an 8-bit CPU. The ISR writes only head and main code writes only tail, so the two never write the same variable.
Incorrect:
#define RB_SIZE 8
typedef struct { volatile uint8_t head, tail; uint8_t buf[RB_SIZE]; } Ring;
void pushBad(Ring *r, uint8_t b) {
  r->buf[r->head] = b;
  r->head = (r->head + 1) & (RB_SIZE - 1);
}
Correct:
#define RB_SIZE 8
typedef struct { volatile uint8_t head, tail; uint8_t buf[RB_SIZE]; } Ring;
int ringPush(Ring *r, uint8_t b) {
  uint8_t next = (r->head + 1) & (RB_SIZE - 1);
  if (next == r->tail) return 0;
  r->buf[r->head] = b;
  r->head = next;
  return 1;
}
int ringPop(Ring *r, uint8_t *b) {
  if (r->head == r->tail) return 0;
  *b = r->buf[r->tail];
  r->tail = (r->tail + 1) & (RB_SIZE - 1);
  return 1;
}
Complexity: O(1)

