Embedded C · AVR_Bare_Metal · non-blocking isr design · access · Advanced
Concept: Read two related ISR variables inside one atomic block so they belong together
Misconception: Reading the sum and then the count gives a consistent average.
Explanation: An ISR adds a sample to a sum and increments a count. If the interrupt fires between the main-code reads of the two variables, the sum is from before the sample and the count is from after it, and the average is wrong. Copy both variables to locals with interrupts off, then compute from the copies.
Incorrect:
volatile uint16_t sumBad;
volatile uint8_t cntBad;
uint16_t avgBad(void (*mid)(void)) {
  uint16_t s = sumBad;
  if (mid != NULL) mid();
  uint8_t c = cntBad;
  return c ? s / c : 0;
}
Correct:
volatile uint16_t sampleSum;
volatile uint8_t sampleCnt;
void sampleIsr(uint16_t v) {
  sampleSum += v;
  sampleCnt++;
}
uint16_t average(void) {
  uint16_t s;
  uint8_t c;
  ATOMIC_BLOCK(ATOMIC_RESTORESTATE) {
    s = sampleSum;
    c = sampleCnt;
  }
  return c ? s / c : 0;
}
Complexity: O(1)

