Context: platform: ESP_IDF
Embedded C · ESP_IDF · lwip networking · transformation · Intermediate
Concept: Pack an IPv4 address and test whether two addresses share a subnet under a prefix length
Misconception: Only the address that is compared against the other needs the netmask.
Explanation: An IPv4 address is a 32-bit value; the first octet goes in the top byte, so 192.168.1.10 is 0xC0A8010A. Two hosts are on the same subnet when their addresses agree after masking, so the mask must be applied to both. A /24 mask is 0xFFFFFF00. Computing a mask as all ones shifted left by (32 - bits) is undefined for bits = 0, because the shift count is 32, so handle that case separately.
Incorrect:
int netBad(uint32_t x, uint32_t y, uint32_t m) { return (x & m) == y; }
Correct:
uint32_t ipPack(uint8_t a, uint8_t b, uint8_t c, uint8_t d) {
  return ((uint32_t)a << 24) | ((uint32_t)b << 16) | ((uint32_t)c << 8) | d;
}
uint32_t maskFromPrefix(int bits) {
  return bits <= 0 ? 0U : 0xFFFFFFFFU << (32 - bits);
}
int sameNet(uint32_t x, uint32_t y, uint32_t mask) {
  return (x & mask) == (y & mask);
}
Complexity: O(1)

