Context: platform: ESP_IDF
Embedded C · ESP_IDF · lwip networking · iteration · Intermediate
Concept: Copy a packet out of a chain of pbufs, crossing segment boundaries and respecting the destination size
Misconception: The first pbuf of a chain holds the whole packet.
Explanation: lwIP stores a packet as a linked chain of pbufs: each has its own payload and len, and the head also holds tot_len for the whole chain. A received frame can be split across several pbufs, for example when it spans memory pool blocks. Reading only p->payload gets the first segment and silently loses the rest. Walk the chain, copy at most the remaining room of the destination at each step, and stop at the end of the chain.
Incorrect:
typedef struct Pb { struct Pb *next; const uint8_t *payload; uint16_t len; } Pb;
size_t pbCopyBad(const Pb *p, uint8_t *dst, size_t max) {
  size_t c = p->len;
  if (c > max) c = max;
  memcpy(dst, p->payload, c);
  return c;
}
Correct:
typedef struct Pb { struct Pb *next; const uint8_t *payload; uint16_t len; } Pb;
size_t pbTotal(const Pb *p) {
  size_t t = 0;
  for (; p; p = p->next) t += p->len;
  return t;
}
size_t pbCopy(const Pb *p, uint8_t *dst, size_t max) {
  size_t k = 0;
  for (; p && k < max; p = p->next) {
    size_t c = p->len;
    if (c > max - k) c = max - k;
    memcpy(dst + k, p->payload, c);
    k += c;
  }
  return k;
}
Complexity: O(n)

