Explanation: A register can change without the program writing it, and a read can have side effects such as clearing a flag. volatile tells the compiler that every access in the source must happen, in order. Without it the optimiser may combine two reads of the same address into one or move a read out of a loop. On Cortex-M the generated code shows it: two reads through a volatile pointer give two load instructions, through a plain pointer only one.
Incorrect:
uint32_t twoReadsBad(const uint32_t *p) {
  uint32_t a = *p;
  uint32_t b = *p;
  return a + b;
}
Correct:
uint32_t twoReads(volatile uint32_t *p) {
  uint32_t a = *p;
  uint32_t b = *p;
  return a + b;
}
Complexity: O(1)

