Explanation: Each interrupt has an 8-bit priority field, but a chip implements only the upper bits (4 on most STM32F4, so values 0 to 15). The value must be shifted left by 8 - 4 = 4. Writing 5 directly stores 0x05, which the hardware reads as priority 0, the highest. A lower number means a higher priority, and an interrupt can preempt only a handler with a numerically larger priority.
Incorrect:
void prioBad(IRQn_Type n, uint32_t p) {
  NVIC->IP[(uint32_t)n] = (uint8_t)p;
}
Correct:
void irqPriority(IRQn_Type n, uint32_t p) {
  NVIC->IP[(uint32_t)n] = (uint8_t)(p << (8U - __NVIC_PRIO_BITS));
}
int canPreempt(uint32_t newPrio, uint32_t runningPrio) {
  return newPrio < runningPrio;
}
Complexity: O(1)

