Context: platform: STM32_HAL
Embedded C · STM32_HAL · pwm timers · complexity · Intermediate
Concept: Pick the smallest prescaler that keeps ARR inside the 16-bit counter for the frequency you want
Misconception: PSC can stay 0 and ARR can be any 32-bit number.
Explanation: General-purpose timers such as TIM3 have a 16-bit auto-reload register, so ARR + 1 can be at most 65536 (TIM2 is 32 bits wide on many parts, check yours). Divide the clock by the frequency to get the total ticks per period and choose the smallest prescaler that brings that under 65536. A small prescaler gives the most steps and so the finest duty resolution. A value that is silently truncated to 16 bits produces a wrong frequency.
Incorrect:
void planBad(uint32_t clk, uint32_t hz, uint32_t *psc, uint32_t *arr) {
  *psc = 0;
  *arr = (uint16_t)(clk / hz - 1U);
}
Correct:
void pwmPlan(uint32_t clk, uint32_t hz, uint32_t *psc, uint32_t *arr) {
  uint32_t ticks = clk / hz;
  *psc = (ticks + 65535U) / 65536U - 1U;
  *arr = ticks / (*psc + 1U) - 1U;
}
Complexity: O(1)

