Context: platform: STM32_HAL
Embedded C · STM32_HAL · stack and heap · iteration · Intermediate
Concept: Replace recursion by a loop when the depth depends on input so stack use stays constant
Misconception: Recursion is safe because the compiler removes the extra stack use.
Explanation: Every recursive call uses a stack frame of its own, often 16 to 64 bytes on a Cortex-M, so a depth that follows the input can exhaust a stack of a few hundred bytes and corrupt whatever lies below it. An iterative version uses the same frame for every step. Where recursion is unavoidable, bound the depth and say what it is.
Incorrect:
static int depth, maxDepth;
long sumRec(int n) {
  depth++;
  if (depth > maxDepth) maxDepth = depth;
  long r = n == 0 ? 0 : n + sumRec(n - 1);
  depth--;
  return r;
}
Correct:
long sumLoop(int n) {
  long s = 0;
  for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) s += i;
  return s;
}
Complexity: O(1)

