Embedded C · STM32_HAL · stack and heap · mental-model · Beginner
Concept: Keep a counter between calls with a static local variable instead of an automatic one
Misconception: A local variable keeps its value from one call to the next.
Explanation: An automatic (ordinary local) variable lives on the stack and is created again on each call. A static local lives in RAM for the whole run of the program, is initialised once, and keeps its value between calls. A function that hands out ids with an automatic counter returns the same id every time.
Incorrect:
int nextIdBad(void) {
  int id = 0;
  return ++id;
}
Correct:
int nextId(void) {
  static int id;
  return ++id;
}
Complexity: O(1)

