Context: platform: STM32_HAL
Embedded C · STM32_HAL · spi master · creation · Intermediate
Concept: Configure SPI1 as master with software slave management, set mode and divider, and enable SPE last
Misconception: MSTR and SPE are enough to run the SPI as a master.
Explanation: With hardware slave management the SPI watches its NSS input; if NSS is low while the peripheral is a master, it flags a mode fault and drops out of master mode. Set SSM and SSI (software management, internal NSS high) and drive the chip select from a normal GPIO. CPOL, CPHA and the baud divider BR must be written while SPE is 0, so enable the peripheral in a separate, final write.
Incorrect:
void spiStartBad(unsigned mode) {
  SPI1->CR1 = SPI_CR1_SPE | SPI_CR1_MSTR | (mode & 3U);
}
Correct:
uint32_t spiCr1(unsigned mode, unsigned br, int lsbFirst) {
  uint32_t v = SPI_CR1_MSTR | SPI_CR1_SSM | SPI_CR1_SSI | ((br & 7U) << 3);
  if (mode & 1U) v |= SPI_CR1_CPHA;
  if (mode & 2U) v |= SPI_CR1_CPOL;
  if (lsbFirst) v |= SPI_CR1_LSBFIRST;
  return v;
}
void spiStart(unsigned mode, unsigned br) {
  SPI1->CR1 = spiCr1(mode, br, 0);
  SPI1->CR1 |= SPI_CR1_SPE;
}
Complexity: O(1)

