Context: platform: STM32_HAL
Embedded C · STM32_HAL · structs and unions · mental-model · Intermediate
Concept: Predict struct size from alignment padding and reduce it by ordering members from largest to smallest
Misconception: The size of a struct is the sum of the sizes of its members.
Explanation: The compiler aligns each member to a multiple of its own size and pads the struct to a multiple of its largest alignment. A uint8_t, a uint32_t and a uint8_t in that order take 12 bytes on a 32-bit ARM core, because 3 bytes of padding follow the first byte and 3 more follow the last. Putting the uint32_t first needs only 8 bytes. Check sizes with sizeof and _Static_assert so a change to the layout breaks the build instead of a protocol.
Incorrect:
typedef struct { uint8_t a; uint32_t b; uint8_t c; } Loose;
size_t looseSum(void) { return sizeof(uint8_t) + sizeof(uint32_t) + sizeof(uint8_t); }
Correct:
typedef struct { uint32_t b; uint8_t a; uint8_t c; } Tight;
_Static_assert(sizeof(Tight) == 8, "Tight must be 8 bytes");
size_t tightSize(void) { return sizeof(Tight); }
size_t tightOffC(void) { return offsetof(Tight, c); }
Complexity: O(1)

