Context: platform: STM32_HAL
Embedded C · STM32_HAL · uart and usart · creation · Intermediate
Concept: Send a byte only when TXE is set, and give the wait a timeout
Misconception: Writing DR is always safe; the hardware queues the byte.
Explanation: The data register holds one byte at a time. If DR is written while TXE is still 0, the previous byte, which has not yet moved to the shift register, is overwritten and lost. Poll TXE (bit 7 of SR) before the write. An unbounded wait hangs the program when the peripheral is not clocked or the clock is wrong, so count down a timeout and report failure.
Incorrect:
void putBad(uint8_t b) {
  USART2->DR = b;
}
Correct:
int uartPutc(uint8_t b, uint32_t spins) {
  while (!(USART2->SR & USART_SR_TXE)) {
    if (spins-- == 0) return -1;
  }
  USART2->DR = b;
  return 0;
}
Complexity: O(1)

