Context: platform: STM32_HAL
Embedded C · STM32_HAL · watchdog timers · access · Intermediate
Concept: Read the reset-cause flag at boot, record it, and then clear the flags
Misconception: After a reset the program cannot tell a watchdog reset from a power-up.
Explanation: The reset and clock control block latches why the last reset happened. On STM32F4 the CSR register has IWDGRSTF (bit 29), WWDGRSTF (bit 30), PORRSTF (bit 27) and others, and RMVF (bit 24) clears them. Read the flags first, save the result, and only then set RMVF; flags that are never cleared stay set across later resets and mislead the next boot. The clearing step is passed in as a function so a test can model what RMVF does.
Incorrect:
int wasWatchdogBad(void (*clearFlags)(void)) {
  clearFlags();
  return (int)((RCC->CSR >> 29) & 1U);
}
Correct:
void rccClearFlags(void) {
  RCC->CSR |= (1U << 24);
}
int wasWatchdog(void (*clearFlags)(void)) {
  int hit = (int)((RCC->CSR >> 29) & 1U);
  clearFlags();
  return hit;
}
Complexity: O(1)

