Context: platform: ESP_IDF
Embedded C · ESP_IDF · wifi station · mental-model · Beginner
Concept: Validate an SSID of 1 to 32 bytes and a WPA2 passphrase of 8 to 63 characters or 64 hex digits
Misconception: The SSID must be shorter than 32 bytes.
Explanation: An SSID may be 1 to 32 bytes, so a 32-byte name is valid. In the ESP-IDF configuration structure the SSID field is exactly 32 bytes and is not guaranteed to end with a zero byte, so copy with a length rather than as a string. A WPA2 passphrase is 8 to 63 printable characters, or exactly 64 hexadecimal digits for a raw key; 64 characters that are not all hex digits are invalid.
Incorrect:
int ssidBad(const char *s) { size_t n = strlen(s); return n >= 1 && n < 32; }
int passBad(const char *p) { size_t n = strlen(p); return n >= 8 && n <= 64; }
Correct:
int ssidOk(const char *s) { size_t n = strlen(s); return n >= 1 && n <= 32; }
int passOk(const char *p) {
  size_t n = strlen(p);
  if (n >= 8 && n <= 63) return 1;
  if (n != 64) return 0;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    char c = p[i];
    int hex = (c >= '0' && c <= '9') || (c >= 'a' && c <= 'f') ||
              (c >= 'A' && c <= 'F');
    if (!hex) return 0;
  }
  return 1;
}
Complexity: O(n)

