Context: platform: STM32_HAL
Embedded C · STM32_HAL · ws2812 led strips · iteration · Intermediate
Concept: Fill the DMA buffer with one compare value per bit, most significant bit first, followed by the reset gap
Misconception: The bits of each colour can be sent in any order as long as all of them are present.
Explanation: With timer PWM and DMA, each bit of the frame is one compare value written into CCR once per period. The buffer holds 24 values per LED, most significant bit first, and then a run of zeros that keeps the line low for the reset gap (at least 50 microseconds, 280 microseconds on newer parts). Least-significant-first order sends the bits of every byte reversed.
Incorrect:
int fillBad(uint16_t *buf, const uint32_t *grb, int n, uint16_t c0, uint16_t c1) {
  int k = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    for (int b = 0; b < 24; b++) buf[k++] = (grb[i] >> b) & 1U ? c1 : c0;
  return k;
}
Correct:
int wsFill(uint16_t *buf, const uint32_t *grb, int n, uint16_t c0, uint16_t c1, int gap) {
  int k = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    for (int b = 23; b >= 0; b--) buf[k++] = (grb[i] >> b) & 1U ? c1 : c0;
  for (int i = 0; i < gap; i++) buf[k++] = 0;
  return k;
}
Complexity: O(n)

