Concept: Take the derivative of the measurement so a setpoint change does not kick the output
Misconception: The derivative of the error is the right input for the D term.
Explanation: When the setpoint jumps, the error jumps with it and the derivative of the error is a huge one-sample spike (derivative kick). Differentiate the measurement instead: since the setpoint is constant between jumps, the result is the same during normal operation and has no spike at a jump.
