Python · searching · patterns · L1
Concept: Two pointers from both ends find a pair in a sorted list; the pointers must stay on different elements
Misconception: The loop can run while i <= j, because a single element might equal half the target.
Explanation: In a sorted list move the left pointer right when the sum is too small and the right pointer left when it is too large. Each step discards one element, so the scan is O(n) with no extra memory. A pair needs two different positions, so the loop runs while i < j; allowing i == j reports an element added to itself.
Incorrect:
def pair_bad(a, target):
    i, j = 0, len(a) - 1
    while i <= j:
        s = a[i] + a[j]
        if s == target:
            return (i, j)
        if s < target:
            i += 1
        else:
            j -= 1
    return None
Correct:
def pair(a, target):
    i, j = 0, len(a) - 1
    while i < j:
        s = a[i] + a[j]
        if s == target:
            return (i, j)
        if s < target:
            i += 1
        else:
            j -= 1
    return None
Complexity: O(n)

