Ruby · graphs stored as adjacency lists · insertion-and-deletion · L1
Concept: An undirected edge must be stored in both adjacency lists, and both endpoints must exist as vertices
Misconception: Adding v to the list of u is enough to connect the two vertices in an undirected graph.
Explanation: With only u -> v stored, v has no way back to u, so searches that start at v miss u and degree counts are wrong. An undirected edge adds v to u's list and u to v's list; a directed edge adds only one. Creating both vertices first keeps isolated endpoints in the vertex list, and neighbors uses fetch so that reading an unknown vertex does not create it. Adding an edge is O(1); space is O(V + E).
Incorrect:
def add_edge_bad(adj, u, v)
  (adj[u] ||= []) << v
  adj[v] ||= []
end
Correct:
class Graph
  def initialize(directed: false)
    @directed = directed
    @adj = {}
  end
  def add_vertex(v)
    @adj[v] ||= []
    self
  end
  def add_edge(u, v)
    add_vertex(u)
    add_vertex(v)
    @adj[u] << v
    @adj[v] << u unless @directed
    self
  end
  def neighbors(v)
    @adj.fetch(v, [])
  end
  def vertices
    @adj.keys
  end
end
Complexity: O(1)

