Ruby · Bloom filters · creation · L2
Concept: Derive the k positions from stable hashes such as Zlib.crc32 and Digest::MD5, never from String#hash
Misconception: item.hash is a quick way to get the hash value for a Bloom filter.
Explanation: String#hash is seeded randomly for every Ruby process, so the same string hashes differently after a restart. A filter that was saved, shared or rebuilt elsewhere then answers wrongly. Use deterministic hashes: Zlib.crc32 and Digest::MD5 give two independent values h1 and h2, and double hashing derives all k positions as (h1 + i * h2) % m. Keep h2 non-zero so positions differ. Each add or lookup costs O(k).
Incorrect:
require "rbconfig"
def process_hash(word)
  IO.popen([RbConfig.ruby, "-e", "print #{word.inspect}.hash"], &:read)
end
Correct:
require "digest"
require "zlib"
class BloomFilter
  attr_reader :m, :k, :bits
  def initialize(m, k)
    @m = m
    @k = k
    @bits = 0
  end
  def positions(item)
    s = item.to_s
    h1 = Zlib.crc32(s)
    h2 = Digest::MD5.hexdigest(s).to_i(16) % (@m - 1) + 1
    Array.new(@k) { |i| (h1 + i * h2) % @m }
  end
  def add(item)
    positions(item).each { |i| @bits |= 1 << i }
    self
  end
  def include?(item)
    positions(item).all? { |i| @bits[i] == 1 }
  end
end
Complexity: O(k)

