Ruby · Bloom filters · access · L2
Concept: A membership query needs all k bits set; one set bit is not enough
Misconception: If any one of the k bits is set the item is probably present.
Explanation: Other items set bits too, so a single set bit is weak evidence. Requiring every one of the k bits makes a false positive need all k positions covered by other items. The answer "no" is always right, because an added item set all of its bits, while "yes" means probably. There are no false negatives unless bits are cleared.
Incorrect:
class BloomAnyBad
  def initialize(filter)
    @f = filter
  end
  def include?(item)
    @f.positions(item).any? { |i| @f.bits[i] == 1 }
  end
end
