document:
Ruby · deques · access · L2
Concept: In a circular buffer every logical index is mapped with modulo, including the back index
Misconception: The last element is at @buf[@head + @size - 1].
Explanation: After the head has moved, head + size - 1 can exceed the last slot and the Array read returns nil instead of wrapping around. Map every logical position with (head + offset) % capacity. In Ruby the modulo of a negative number is non-negative, so (head - 1) % capacity works for push_front. All end operations are O(1).
Incorrect:
class RingBad
  def initialize(cap)
    @buf = Array.new(cap)
    @head = 0
    @size = 0
  end
  def push_back(v)
    @buf[(@head + @size) % @buf.size] = v
    @size += 1
  end
  def pop_front
    v = @buf[@head]
    @head = (@head + 1) % @buf.size
    @size -= 1
    v
  end
  def back
    @buf[@head + @size - 1]
  end
end
Correct:
class RingDeque
  include Enumerable
  attr_reader :size
  def initialize(cap = 4)
    @buf = Array.new(cap)
    @head = 0
    @size = 0
  end
  def capacity
    @buf.size
  end
  def full?
    @size == @buf.size
  end
  def empty?
    @size.zero?
  end
  def push_back(v)
    return false if full?
    @buf[(@head + @size) % @buf.size] = v
    @size += 1
    true
  end
  def push_front(v)
    return false if full?
    @head = (@head - 1) % @buf.size
    @buf[@head] = v
    @size += 1
    true
  end
  def pop_front
    return nil if empty?
    v = @buf[@head]
    @buf[@head] = nil
    @head = (@head + 1) % @buf.size
    @size -= 1
    v
  end
  def pop_back
    return nil if empty?
    i = (@head + @size - 1) % @buf.size
    v = @buf[i]
    @buf[i] = nil
    @size -= 1
    v
  end
  def front
    empty? ? nil : @buf[@head]
  end
  def back
    empty? ? nil : @buf[(@head + @size - 1) % @buf.size]
  end
  def each
    return to_enum(:each) unless block_given?
    @size.times { |i| yield @buf[(@head + i) % @buf.size] }
  end
end
Complexity: O(1)

