Ruby · disjoint sets (union-find) · creation · L1
Concept: Every element starts as its own parent: parent[i] = i, not a repeated default
Misconception: Array.new(n, 0) is a fine initial parent array.
Explanation: With every parent set to 0, all elements start in the set of element 0, so every pair looks connected before any union. A root is defined by parent[x] == x, so the initial array must be the identity: Array.new(n) { |i| i }. Then n elements form n sets, find walks parents up to the root, and union links one root to the other. The count of sets starts at n and drops by one per successful union.
Incorrect:
def find_bad(parent, x)
  x = parent[x] while parent[x] != x
  x
end
Correct:
class DisjointSet
  attr_reader :parent, :rank, :count
  def initialize(n)
    @parent = Array.new(n) { |i| i }
    @rank = Array.new(n, 0)
    @count = n
  end
  def find(x)
    x = @parent[x] while @parent[x] != x
    x
  end
  def union(a, b)
    ra = find(a)
    rb = find(b)
    return false if ra == rb
    @parent[ra] = rb
    @count -= 1
    true
  end
  def connected?(a, b)
    find(a) == find(b)
  end
end
Complexity: O(h)

