Ruby · priority queues · ordering · L3
Concept: A heap is not stable; add an insertion counter to break priority ties first-in, first-out
Misconception: Items with equal priority leave the queue in the order they were inserted.
Explanation: Heap operations swap elements across the array, so equal-priority items come out in an arbitrary order. Store [priority, sequence, value] and compare arrays: Array#<=> compares element by element, so the unique sequence number settles every tie and the value is never compared. Cost is one extra Integer per item.
Incorrect:
Job = Struct.new(:pri, :name)
def jobs_bad(names)
  q = PQ.new { |a, b| a.pri <=> b.pri }
  names.each { |n| q.push(Job.new(1, n)) }
  Array.new(names.size) { q.pop.name }
end
Correct:
class StablePQ
  def initialize
    @q = PQ.new { |a, b| [a[0], a[1]] <=> [b[0], b[1]] }
    @seq = 0
  end
  def push(priority, value)
    @seq += 1
    @q.push([priority, @seq, value])
    self
  end
  def pop
    item = @q.pop
    item && item[2]
  end
  def size
    @q.size
  end
end
Complexity: O(log n)

