Ruby · queues · mutation · L3
Concept: A ring buffer queue wraps its indexes with modulo so storage stays fixed
Misconception: Advancing head and tail with += 1 is enough for a fixed-size queue.
Explanation: Without modulo the indexes grow forever, and assigning past the end of a Ruby Array silently extends it, so the supposedly fixed buffer keeps growing. Wrap with (i + 1) % capacity. Track size separately to tell full from empty. enqueue and dequeue are O(1) with constant memory.
Incorrect:
class RingBad
  def initialize(cap)
    @buf = Array.new(cap)
    @cap = cap
    @head = @tail = @size = 0
  end
  def slots
    @buf.size
  end
  def enqueue(v)
    return false if @size == @cap
    @buf[@tail] = v
    @tail += 1
    @size += 1
    true
  end
  def dequeue
    return nil if @size.zero?
    v = @buf[@head]
    @head += 1
    @size -= 1
    v
  end
end
Correct:
class RingQueue
  def initialize(cap)
    @buf = Array.new(cap)
    @head = @tail = @size = 0
  end
  def slots
    @buf.size
  end
  def enqueue(v)
    return false if @size == @buf.size
    @buf[@tail] = v
    @tail = (@tail + 1) % @buf.size
    @size += 1
    true
  end
  def dequeue
    return nil if @size.zero?
    v = @buf[@head]
    @buf[@head] = nil
    @head = (@head + 1) % @buf.size
    @size -= 1
    v
