Ruby · Range · creation · L1
Concept: a..b includes b and a...b excludes it; index loops over an array need the exclusive form
Misconception: (0..a.size) is the right range for visiting every index of an array.
Explanation: The two-dot range includes its end, the three-dot range stops before it. The valid indexes of an array are 0...a.size, so (0..a.size) visits one extra index, where a[i] is nil. (1..5).size is 5 and (1...5).size is 4. For simple loops prefer each_index or each_with_index.
Incorrect:
def doubled_bad(a)
  (0..a.size).map { |i| a[i] * 2 }
end
Correct:
def doubled(a)
  (0...a.size).map { |i| a[i] * 2 }
end
Complexity: O(n)

