TypeScript · array · creation · L1
Concept: new Array(n).fill([]) puts one array in every slot; build rows with Array.from
Misconception: new Array(3).fill([]) creates three independent empty rows.
Explanation: fill stores the same value in every slot. For a primitive that is fine; for an array or object all slots point to one object, so pushing to one row changes all rows. Array.from({ length: n }, () => []) runs the callback once per slot and creates separate rows. Annotate the element type, because [] alone is inferred as never[].
Incorrect:
function gridBad(n: number): number[][] {
  return new Array<number[]>(n).fill([]);
}
Correct:
function grid(n: number): number[][] {
  return Array.from({ length: n }, () => [] as number[]);
}
Complexity: O(n)

