TypeScript · array · patterns · L3
Concept: A generic helper that may find nothing must return T | undefined, not T
Misconception: function first<T>(xs: T[]): T is correctly typed, because the element type is T.
Explanation: Reading xs[0] is typed T, but an empty array returns undefined, so the declared return type lies and callers skip the check. Declare T | undefined, or take a non-empty tuple type [T, ...T[]] when the caller guarantees one element. The compiler then forces the check at every call site.
Incorrect:
function firstBad<T>(xs: T[]): T {
  return xs[0] as T;
}
Correct:
function first<T>(xs: readonly T[]): T | undefined {
  return xs[0];
}
function head<T>(xs: readonly [T, ...T[]]): T {
  return xs[0];
}
Complexity: O(1)

