TypeScript · array · mutation · L1
Concept: readonly number[] removes the mutating methods from the type, so a function can promise not to change its argument
Misconception: A function that takes number[] and does not change it is as safe as one that takes readonly number[].
Explanation: A parameter typed number[] allows push, sort and splice, so callers cannot tell whether their array survives the call. readonly number[] (or ReadonlyArray<number>) hides the mutating methods, and the compiler rejects them. A mutable array can be passed where readonly is expected, but not the other way round. Return a new array instead of changing the input.
Incorrect (compile error TS2339):
function addBad(xs: readonly number[]): void {
  xs.push(1);
}
Correct:
function add(xs: readonly number[], x: number): number[] {
  return [...xs, x];
}
Complexity: O(n)

