TypeScript · array · ordering · L1
Concept: sort() compares as strings by default and sorts in place; pass a numeric comparator or use toSorted()
Misconception: nums.sort() sorts a number[] numerically, since the compiler knows the elements are numbers.
Explanation: The default comparator converts elements to strings, so 10 sorts before 9. TypeScript accepts it without complaint. Pass (a, b) => a - b for numbers. sort mutates the array and returns it; toSorted (ES2023) returns a sorted copy and leaves the original alone.
Incorrect:
function sortBad(nums: number[]): number[] {
  return nums.sort();
}
Correct:
function sortNumbers(nums: readonly number[]): number[] {
  return nums.toSorted((a, b) => a - b);
}
Complexity: O(n log n)

