Concept: A view needs the buffer length to be a multiple of its element size, or the constructor throws RangeError
Misconception: new Uint32Array(buf) works for any buffer length.
Explanation: Each Uint32 element takes four bytes, so a six-byte buffer cannot be covered and the constructor throws RangeError. Pass an explicit length that fits, new Uint32Array(buf, 0, buf.byteLength >> 2), and handle the leftover bytes yourself. A non-zero byteOffset must also be a multiple of the element size.
Incorrect:
function asU32Bad(buf: ArrayBuffer): Uint32Array {
  return new Uint32Array(buf);
}
Correct:
function asU32(buf: ArrayBuffer): Uint32Array {
  return new Uint32Array(buf, 0, buf.byteLength >> 2);
}
Complexity: O(1)

