document:
TypeScript · arraybuffer · creation · L1
Concept: ArrayBuffer.slice copies the bytes; to change the original, write through a view over it
Misconception: Writing to buf.slice(0, 4) changes the first four bytes of buf.
Explanation: slice returns a new buffer holding a copy of the range. Writes to the copy never reach the original. To update a region in place, create a view with an offset and length, new Uint8Array(buf, 0, 4), and write to that.
Incorrect:
function zeroHeaderBad(buf: ArrayBuffer): void {
  new Uint8Array(buf.slice(0, 4)).fill(0);
}
Correct:
function zeroHeader(buf: ArrayBuffer): void {
  new Uint8Array(buf, 0, 4).fill(0);
}
Complexity: O(n)

