document:
TypeScript · avltree · testing · L3
Concept: A balance checker must recompute heights from the shape, not trust the stored values
Misconception: Comparing stored height fields proves the tree is balanced.
Explanation: Stored heights are exactly what a bug corrupts, so a checker that reads them agrees with the bug. Recompute the height of each subtree from the structure and return a sentinel such as -1 as soon as any node differs by more than one.
Incorrect:
function isAvlBad<K>(n: AvlNode<K> | null): boolean {
  if (n === null) return true;
  const diff = Math.abs(hOf(n.left) - hOf(n.right));
  return diff <= 1 && isAvlBad(n.left) && isAvlBad(n.right);
}
Correct:
function checkAvl<K>(n: AvlNode<K> | null): number {
  if (n === null) return 0;
  const l = checkAvl(n.left);
  const r = checkAvl(n.right);
  if (l < 0 || r < 0 || Math.abs(l - r) > 1) return -1;
  return 1 + Math.max(l, r);
}
Complexity: O(n)

