TypeScript · avltree · insertion-and-deletion · L4
Concept: Delete can unbalance an ancestor far above the removed node, so rebalance after every recursive call
Misconception: Only inserts need rotations; a delete can only shorten a tree.
Explanation: Removing a node shortens one side of its ancestors, which can make a sibling side too tall by two. After the recursive removal (including the successor removal) every ancestor must be rebalanced. A delete may need several rotations along the path, where an insert needs at most one.
Incorrect:
function removeBad<K>(n: AvlNode<K> | null, key: K, cmp: Cmp<K>): AvlNode<K> | null {
  if (n === null) return null;
  const c = cmp(key, n.key);
  if (c < 0) n.left = removeBad(n.left, key, cmp);
  else if (c > 0) n.right = removeBad(n.right, key, cmp);
  else {
    if (n.left === null) return n.right;
    if (n.right === null) return n.left;
    let s = n.right;
    while (s.left !== null) s = s.left;
    n.key = s.key;
    n.right = removeBad(n.right, s.key, cmp);
  }
  fix(n);
  return n;
}
Correct:
function remove<K>(n: AvlNode<K> | null, key: K, cmp: Cmp<K>): AvlNode<K> | null {
  if (n === null) return null;
  const c = cmp(key, n.key);
  if (c < 0) n.left = remove(n.left, key, cmp);
  else if (c > 0) n.right = remove(n.right, key, cmp);
  else {
    if (n.left === null) return n.right;
    if (n.right === null) return n.left;
    let s = n.right;
    while (s.left !== null) s = s.left;
    n.key = s.key;
    n.right = remove(n.right, s.key, cmp);
  }
  return rebalance(n);
}
Complexity: O(log n)

