TypeScript · binarytree · patterns · L3
Concept: The diameter may avoid the root, so track a best value at every node
Misconception: The longest path goes through the root: height(left) + height(right).
Explanation: The longest path between two nodes may lie entirely inside one subtree. Compute depths bottom up and, at every node, update a running best with leftDepth + rightDepth, while returning 1 + the larger depth to the parent. A closure variable holds the best.
Incorrect:
function diameterBad<T>(root: TreeNode<T> | null): number {
  const depth = (n: TreeNode<T> | null): number =>
    n === null ? 0 : 1 + Math.max(depth(n.left), depth(n.right));
  return root === null ? 0 : depth(root.left) + depth(root.right);
}
Correct:
function diameter<T>(root: TreeNode<T> | null): number {
  let best = 0;
  const depth = (n: TreeNode<T> | null): number => {
    if (n === null) return 0;
    const l = depth(n.left);
    const r = depth(n.right);
    best = Math.max(best, l + r);
    return 1 + Math.max(l, r);
  };
  depth(root);
  return best;
}
Complexity: O(n)

