TypeScript · binarytree · edge-cases · L1
Concept: Height adds one for the current node; taking the max of the children alone never grows
Misconception: height(n) = max(height(left), height(right)).
Explanation: The height of a node is one more than the taller child. Without the + 1 every call returns the base case value. Define the empty tree as -1 so a leaf has height 0 (edges), and state the convention because node-count heights use 0 for an empty tree.
Incorrect:
function heightBad<T>(n: TreeNode<T> | null): number {
  return n === null ? -1 : Math.max(heightBad(n.left), heightBad(n.right));
}
Correct:
function height<T>(n: TreeNode<T> | null): number {
  return n === null ? -1 : 1 + Math.max(height(n.left), height(n.right));
}
Complexity: O(n)

