TypeScript · binarytree · iteration · L2
Concept: Array.prototype.concat returns a new array; calling it for its side effect loses the subtree
Misconception: out.concat(inorder(n.left)) appends the left subtree to out.
Explanation: concat does not modify the array it is called on, it returns a new one. A traversal that calls it and ignores the result returns only the root value. Spread the results into a new array, or push into one shared array.
Incorrect:
function inorderBad<T>(n: TreeNode<T> | null): T[] {
  if (n === null) return [];
  const out: T[] = [];
  out.concat(inorderBad(n.left));
  out.push(n.val);
  out.concat(inorderBad(n.right));
  return out;
}
Correct:
function inorder<T>(n: TreeNode<T> | null): T[] {
  if (n === null) return [];
  return [...inorder(n.left), n.val, ...inorder(n.right)];
}
Complexity: O(n)

