TypeScript · bloomfilter · mutation · L2
Concept: Clearing bits to delete an item can break other items; use a counting Bloom filter
Misconception: Setting an item bits back to 0 removes it from the filter.
Explanation: Other items may share those bits, and clearing them makes those items disappear, which is a false negative, the one error a Bloom filter must never make. A counting Bloom filter keeps a small counter per position: add increments, remove decrements, and a position counts as set while its counter is above zero.
Incorrect:
function removeBad(f: BloomFilter, item: string): void {
  for (const i of indexes(item, f.m, f.k)) f.bits[i] = 0;
}
Correct:
class CountingBloom {
  counts: Uint8Array;
  constructor(readonly m: number, readonly k: number) { this.counts = new Uint8Array(m); }
  add(item: string): void {
    for (const i of indexes(item, this.m, this.k)) this.counts[i]!++;
  }
  remove(item: string): void {
    if (!this.has(item)) return;
    for (const i of indexes(item, this.m, this.k)) this.counts[i]!--;
  }
  has(item: string): boolean {
    return indexes(item, this.m, this.k).every((i) => (this.counts[i] as number) > 0);
  }
}
Complexity: O(k)

