document:
TypeScript · bloomfilter · access · L1
Concept: A Bloom filter says "possibly present" or "definitely absent"; a true answer must be confirmed
Misconception: has(x) returning true proves that x was added.
Explanation: Bits set by other items can cover every position of an item that was never added, which is a false positive. A false answer is always correct (no false negatives) but a true answer is only probable. Use the filter as a cheap pre-check in front of an authoritative lookup, such as a Set or a database.
Incorrect:
function isMemberBad(f: BloomFilter, x: string): boolean {
  return f.has(x);
}
Correct:
function isMember(f: BloomFilter, truth: ReadonlySet<string>, x: string): boolean {
  return f.has(x) && truth.has(x);
}
Complexity: O(k)

