TypeScript · bloomfilter · patterns · L3
Concept: Merge two filters of the same size with bitwise OR; AND would drop members
Misconception: The union of two filters is the bitwise AND of their bit arrays.
Explanation: An item is in the union if it is in either filter, so a position must be set when it is set in either: OR. AND keeps only positions set in both, so items that were in just one filter become false negatives. Both filters must use the same m, k and hash functions.
Incorrect:
function mergeBad(a: BloomFilter, b: BloomFilter): BloomFilter {
  const out = new BloomFilter(a.m, a.k);
  out.bits = a.bits.map((v, i) => v & (b.bits[i] as number));
  return out;
}
Correct:
function merge(a: BloomFilter, b: BloomFilter): BloomFilter {
  if (a.m !== b.m || a.k !== b.k) throw new Error("incompatible filters");
  const out = new BloomFilter(a.m, a.k);
  out.bits = a.bits.map((v, i) => v | (b.bits[i] as number));
  return out;
}
Complexity: O(m)

