document:
TypeScript · bst · edge-cases · L2
Concept: A set-like tree ignores an equal key; inserting duplicates inflates the size
Misconception: Equal keys can go to the left subtree.
Explanation: Decide the duplicate policy up front. A set keeps one copy: when the comparator returns 0 the insert does nothing. Sending equal keys left stores each copy, so the count grows with every repeated insert and a single remove leaves the key present. If counts matter, store a count in the node.
Incorrect:
function insertDupBad<K>(n: BNode<K> | null, key: K, cmp: Cmp<K>): BNode<K> {
  if (n === null) return new BNode(key);
  if (cmp(key, n.key) <= 0) n.left = insertDupBad(n.left, key, cmp);
  else n.right = insertDupBad(n.right, key, cmp);
  return n;
}
Correct:
function has<K>(n: BNode<K> | null, key: K, cmp: Cmp<K>): boolean {
  let cur = n;
  while (cur !== null) {
    const c = cmp(key, cur.key);
    if (c === 0) return true;
    cur = c < 0 ? cur.left : cur.right;
  }
  return false;
}
Complexity: O(h)

