TypeScript · bst · iteration · L3
Concept: A recursive generator must use yield* to forward the values of its child generators
Misconception: Calling the child generator inside the generator yields its values.
Explanation: Calling a generator function only creates an iterator object and runs nothing. Without yield* the values of the left and right subtrees are never produced, so the generator yields only the root. Use yield* to delegate to the child generators.
Incorrect:
function* keysBad<K>(n: BNode<K> | null): Generator<K> {
  if (n === null) return;
  keysBad(n.left);
  yield n.key;
  keysBad(n.right);
}
Correct:
function* keys<K>(n: BNode<K> | null): Generator<K> {
  if (n === null) return;
  yield* keys(n.left);
  yield n.key;
  yield* keys(n.right);
}
Complexity: O(n)

