TypeScript · bst · complexity · L2
Concept: Inserting sorted keys one by one builds a chain; build from the sorted middle instead
Misconception: A BST is balanced no matter what order keys are inserted in.
Explanation: Inserting keys in ascending order makes every new key a right child, so the tree becomes a linked list with height n - 1 and every operation is O(n). To build a balanced tree from sorted data, take the middle as the root and recurse on each half. Self-balancing trees fix this on every insert.
Incorrect:
function fromSortedBad(keys: number[]): BNode<number> | null {
  let root: BNode<number> | null = null;
  for (const k of keys) root = insert(root, k, numCmp);
  return root;
}
Correct:
function fromSorted(keys: number[], lo = 0, hi = keys.length - 1): BNode<number> | null {
  if (lo > hi) return null;
  const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
  return new BNode(
    keys[mid] as number, fromSorted(keys, lo, mid - 1), fromSorted(keys, mid + 1, hi),
  );
}
Complexity: O(n)

