TypeScript · btree · complexity · L1
Concept: The height is about log base t of n, so a wide node keeps the tree very shallow
Misconception: A search tree with n keys has height about log2 n.
Explanation: A node with at least t children divides the keys by t at each level, so the height is at most log base t of (n + 1) / 2. With t = 50 and a million keys the height is 3 edges, not 19. Fewer levels means fewer disk reads, which is why databases use B-trees.
Incorrect:
function maxHeightBad(n: number, t: number): number {
  return Math.floor(Math.log2(n));
}
Correct:
function maxHeight(n: number, t: number): number {
  return Math.floor(Math.log((n + 1) / 2) / Math.log(t) + 1e-9);
}
Complexity: O(1)

