TypeScript · dataview · patterns · L3
Concept: Encode and decode a binary header with named offsets and one endianness; mixing flags corrupts fields
Misconception: Writing little-endian and reading with the default flag round-trips correctly.
Explanation: Every multi-byte read and write must use the same endianness. A good layout defines constants for each field offset and size, plus one LITTLE flag used everywhere. Decoding a little-endian field with the default big-endian read gives a different number.
Incorrect:
function decodeBad(buf: ArrayBuffer): { type: number; length: number } {
  const dv = new DataView(buf);
  return { type: dv.getUint8(0), length: dv.getUint16(1) };
}
Correct:
const LITTLE = true;
function encode(type: number, length: number): ArrayBuffer {
  const buf = new ArrayBuffer(3);
  const dv = new DataView(buf);
  dv.setUint8(0, type);
  dv.setUint16(1, length, LITTLE);
  return buf;
}
function decode(buf: ArrayBuffer): { type: number; length: number } {
  const dv = new DataView(buf);
  return { type: dv.getUint8(0), length: dv.getUint16(1, LITTLE) };
}
Complexity: O(1)

