TypeScript · dataview · transformation · L2
Concept: Strings are not bytes: use TextEncoder, because string length counts UTF-16 units, not UTF-8 bytes
Misconception: A string of length n needs n bytes, and charCodeAt gives each byte.
Explanation: Non-ASCII text takes several bytes in UTF-8: "é" has length 1 but is two bytes. Allocating s.length bytes truncates the data, and writing charCodeAt values stores UTF-16 code units. Use new TextEncoder().encode(s) to get the bytes and TextDecoder to read them.
Incorrect:
function packBad(s: string): Uint8Array {
  const out = new Uint8Array(s.length);
  for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) out[i] = s.charCodeAt(i);
  return out;
}
Correct:
function pack(s: string): Uint8Array {
  return new TextEncoder().encode(s);
}
function unpack(b: Uint8Array): string {
  return new TextDecoder().decode(b);
}
Complexity: O(n)

