document:
TypeScript · date · transformation · L3
Concept: Day differences by dividing milliseconds by 86,400,000 break across daylight saving changes
Misconception: A day is always 24 hours, so ms / 86400000 counts days.
Explanation: In zones with daylight saving, a local day can be 23 or 25 hours. Dividing the millisecond difference between two local midnights gives fractions such as 1.96. Convert both dates to UTC midnight with Date.UTC(year, month, day) and round the quotient.
Incorrect:
function daysBetweenBad(a: Date, b: Date): number {
  return Math.floor((b.getTime() - a.getTime()) / 86_400_000);
}
Correct:
function daysBetween(a: Date, b: Date): number {
  const ua = Date.UTC(a.getFullYear(), a.getMonth(), a.getDate());
  const ub = Date.UTC(b.getFullYear(), b.getMonth(), b.getDate());
  return Math.round((ub - ua) / 86_400_000);
}
Complexity: O(1)

