TypeScript · disjointset · complexity · L2
Concept: Path compression points every visited node at the root so later finds are nearly constant
Misconception: Walking to the root without compression keeps find fast.
Explanation: Without compression a chain of unions can leave a path of length n, and every find walks it. Compression rewrites each node on the path to point directly at the root, flattening the tree. Combined with union by size it gives the inverse-Ackermann bound, which is practically constant.
Incorrect:
function findSlow(parent: number[], x: number): number {
  while (parent[x] !== x) x = parent[x] as number;
  return x;
}
Correct:
function findCompress(parent: number[], x: number): number {
  let root = x;
  while (parent[root] !== root) root = parent[root] as number;
  while (parent[x] !== root) {
    const next = parent[x] as number;
    parent[x] = root;
    x = next;
  }
  return root;
}
Complexity: O(alpha(n))

