TypeScript · doublylinkedlist · insertion-and-deletion · L2
Concept: Removing a node in O(1) must handle the head and tail, where prev or next is null
Misconception: node.prev.next = node.next works for every node.
Explanation: The first node has no prev and the last has no next, so those assignments throw TypeError. Branch on each side: update the neighbour if there is one, otherwise move the list head or tail. Also clear the removed node links so it cannot be reused by accident.
Incorrect:
type DNode<T> = { value: T; prev: DNode<T> | null; next: DNode<T> | null };
type DLike<T> = { head: DNode<T> | null; tail: DNode<T> | null; size: number };
function removeNodeBad<T>(l: DLike<T>, n: DNode<T>): void {
  n.prev!.next = n.next;
  n.next!.prev = n.prev;
  l.size--;
}
Correct:
type DNode<T> = { value: T; prev: DNode<T> | null; next: DNode<T> | null };
type DLike<T> = { head: DNode<T> | null; tail: DNode<T> | null; size: number };
function removeNode<T>(l: DLike<T>, n: DNode<T>): void {
  if (n.prev) n.prev.next = n.next;
  else l.head = n.next;
  if (n.next) n.next.prev = n.prev;
  else l.tail = n.prev;
  n.prev = n.next = null;
  l.size--;
}
Complexity: O(1)

