TypeScript · doublylinkedlist · mutation · L2
Concept: Reversing swaps prev and next on every node and also swaps head and tail
Misconception: Swapping prev and next on each node is enough to reverse the list.
Explanation: After the swaps the old tail is the first node, so head and tail must also be exchanged. Forgetting that leaves a list whose head points at the old first node, whose next is now null. Walk with the saved next pointer, because swapping changes which field goes forward.
Incorrect:
type DNode<T> = { value: T; prev: DNode<T> | null; next: DNode<T> | null };
type DLike<T> = { head: DNode<T> | null; tail: DNode<T> | null; size: number };
function reverseBad<T>(l: DLike<T>): void {
  for (let n = l.head; n; n = n.prev) [n.prev, n.next] = [n.next, n.prev];
}
Correct:
type DNode<T> = { value: T; prev: DNode<T> | null; next: DNode<T> | null };
type DLike<T> = { head: DNode<T> | null; tail: DNode<T> | null; size: number };
function reverse<T>(l: DLike<T>): void {
  let n = l.head;
  while (n) {
    const next = n.next;
    [n.prev, n.next] = [n.next, n.prev];
    n = next;
  }
  [l.head, l.tail] = [l.tail, l.head];
}
Complexity: O(n)

