TypeScript · enum · fit-and-alternatives · L2
Concept: An as const object plus a derived union gives enum-like safety with plain JavaScript output
Misconception: Any string can be assigned to a type derived from an as const object.
Explanation: type Dir = (typeof DIR)[keyof typeof DIR] is the union "UP" | "DOWN". Assigning "LEFT" is error TS2322, and no extra runtime code is generated beyond the object. This style works with erasable-syntax-only settings and with plain JavaScript tooling.
Incorrect (compile error TS2322):
const DIRB = { Up: "UP", Down: "DOWN" } as const;
type DirTypeB = (typeof DIRB)[keyof typeof DIRB];
const badDir: DirTypeB = "LEFT";
Correct:
const DIR = { Up: "UP", Down: "DOWN" } as const;
type DirType = (typeof DIR)[keyof typeof DIR];
function move(d: DirType): string {
  return d === DIR.Up ? "+1" : "-1";
}
Complexity: O(1)

