TypeScript · fenwicktree · creation · L3
Concept: Build in linear time by pushing each node into its parent; copying the array is not a tree
Misconception: A Fenwick tree can be created by copying the input array.
Explanation: Each tree node holds a partial sum, so the raw array is not a valid tree. Calling add for every element costs O(n log n). A linear build puts the values at indexes 1..n, then adds each node into its parent at i + lowbit(i) once.
Incorrect:
function fromArrayBad(a: number[]): number[] {
  return [...a];
}
Correct:
function fromArray(a: number[]): number[] {
  const n = a.length;
  const tree = [0, ...a];
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    const parent = i + (i & -i);
    if (parent <= n) tree[parent] = (tree[parent] as number) + (tree[i] as number);
  }
  return tree;
}
Complexity: O(n)

