TypeScript · fenwicktree · patterns · L3
Concept: Compress values to ranks with a numeric sort before counting inversions
Misconception: Sorting the distinct values with sort() gives their ranks.
Explanation: Counting inversions uses a Fenwick tree indexed by rank. The default sort compares strings, so 10 comes before 9 and the ranks are wrong. Sort with (x, y) => x - y, map each value to its rank, and for each element count the earlier elements with a larger rank as i minus prefix(rank).
Incorrect:
function inversionsBad(a: number[]): number {
  const sorted = [...new Set(a)].sort();
  const rank = new Map<number, number>(sorted.map((v, i) => [v, i]));
  const f = new Fenwick(sorted.length);
  let inv = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
    const r = rank.get(a[i] as number) as number;
    inv += i - f.prefix(r);
    f.add(r, 1);
  }
  return inv;
}
Correct:
function inversions(a: number[]): number {
  const sorted = [...new Set(a)].sort((x, y) => x - y);
  const rank = new Map<number, number>(sorted.map((v, i) => [v, i]));
  const f = new Fenwick(sorted.length);
  let inv = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
    const r = rank.get(a[i] as number) as number;
    inv += i - f.prefix(r);
    f.add(r, 1);
  }
  return inv;
}
Complexity: O(n log n)

