TypeScript · fenwicktree · mutation · L3
Concept: Setting a value means adding the difference from the stored value, not the new value
Misconception: set(i, v) can call add(i, v).
Explanation: A Fenwick tree stores sums, so add always accumulates. To replace a value, keep a plain array of the current values and add v minus the old value. Calling add with the new value doubles the element on a repeated set.
Incorrect:
class SetBad extends Fenwick {
  set(i: number, v: number): void {
    this.add(i, v);
  }
}
Correct:
class FenwickSet extends Fenwick {
  vals: number[];
  constructor(n: number) {
    super(n);
    this.vals = new Array<number>(n).fill(0);
  }
  set(i: number, v: number): void {
    this.add(i, v - (this.vals[i] as number));
    this.vals[i] = v;
  }
}
Complexity: O(log n)

