TypeScript · fenwicktree · mutation · L2
Concept: An update walks up by adding the lowbit, touching every node whose range covers the index
Misconception: An update can walk down with x -= x & -x, like a query.
Explanation: A query walks down, removing the lowest bit each step, to collect disjoint ranges. An update must reach every node whose range contains the index, which means walking up with x += x & -x. Walking down updates only the nodes below the index and later prefix sums miss the change.
Incorrect:
function addBad(tree: number[], i: number, d: number): void {
  for (let x = i + 1; x > 0; x -= x & -x) tree[x] = (tree[x] as number) + d;
}
Correct:
class Fenwick {
  tree: number[];
  constructor(readonly n: number) { this.tree = new Array<number>(n + 1).fill(0); }
  add(i: number, delta: number): void {
    for (let x = i + 1; x <= this.n; x += x & -x) this.tree[x]! += delta;
  }
  prefix(i: number): number {
    let sum = 0;
    for (let x = i + 1; x > 0; x -= x & -x) sum += this.tree[x] as number;
    return sum;
  }
}
Complexity: O(log n)

