TypeScript · hashtable · equality · L2
Concept: Compare keys with strict equality (and a NaN rule); loose == makes 1 and "1" the same key
Misconception: Keys can be compared with == because both are hashed to the same string.
Explanation: Loose equality converts types, so the number 1 and the string "1" are equal and one overwrites the other. Strict equality keeps them separate. NaN is never equal to itself, so a table that must find NaN needs a SameValueZero rule: a === b or both are NaN.
Incorrect:
function getLoose<V>(t: Table<string | number, V>, k: string | number): V | undefined {
  const chain = t.buckets[indexOf(k, t.buckets.length)] as [string | number, V][];
  return chain.find(([key]) => key == k)?.[1];
}
Correct:
function getStrict<V>(t: Table<string | number, V>, k: string | number): V | undefined {
  const chain = t.buckets[indexOf(k, t.buckets.length)] as [string | number, V][];
  return chain.find(([key]) => key === k || (key !== key && k !== k))?.[1];
}
Complexity: O(1) average

