TypeScript · interface · creation · L2
Concept: An interface can describe a function with properties; a plain function is missing them (TS2741)
Misconception: A function can be assigned to an interface that has a call signature and extra members.
Explanation: interface Counter { (): number; reset(): void } describes a callable object. A plain arrow function is missing reset (error TS2741). Build it with Object.assign(fn, { reset }), so the function and its members form one value.
Incorrect (compile error TS2741):
interface CounterB {
  (): number;
  reset(): void;
}
const cBad: CounterB = () => 1;
Correct:
interface Counter {
  (): number;
  reset(): void;
}
function makeCounter(): Counter {
  let n = 0;
  return Object.assign(() => ++n, { reset: () => { n = 0; } });
}
Complexity: O(1)

