TypeScript · interface · patterns · L3
Concept: A generic interface describes a data structure once, and the compiler rejects wrong element types
Misconception: A Stack<number> accepts any value that can be converted to a number.
Explanation: Stack<T> fixes the element type: pushing a string into a Stack<number> is error TS2345. An interface lets several classes implement the same contract and lets tests use a simple fake.
Incorrect (compile error TS2345):
interface StackB<T> {
  push(x: T): void;
  pop(): T | undefined;
}
function fillBad(s: StackB<number>): void {
  s.push("a");
}
Correct:
interface Stack<T> {
  push(x: T): void;
  pop(): T | undefined;
}
class ArrayStack<T> implements Stack<T> {
  private items: T[] = [];
  push(x: T): void { this.items.push(x); }
  pop(): T | undefined { return this.items.pop(); }
}
Complexity: O(1)

