TypeScript · interface · creation · L2
Concept: Interfaces with the same name merge; a type alias declared twice is error TS2300
Misconception: Declaring the same name twice always replaces the earlier declaration.
Explanation: Two interface declarations of the same name in one scope are merged into one type with all members, which is how libraries are augmented. Two type aliases with the same name are error TS2300 (duplicate identifier). Accidental merging can also hide a typo, so keep declarations together.
Incorrect (compile error TS2300):
type PointB = { x: number };
type PointB = { y: number };
Correct:
interface Point { x: number }
interface Point { y: number }
function norm(p: Point): number {
  return Math.hypot(p.x, p.y);
}
Complexity: O(1)

