TypeScript · json · ordering · L2
Concept: Parsed objects list integer-like keys first; keep order-sensitive data as an array of pairs
Misconception: JSON.parse preserves the order of keys in the text.
Explanation: JSON objects are unordered by specification, and JavaScript objects put integer-like keys first in ascending order. {"b":1,"2":1} parses to keys ["2","b"]. When order matters, store an array of [key, value] pairs or an array of objects.
Incorrect:
function firstKeyBad(s: string): string | undefined {
  return Object.keys(JSON.parse(s))[0];
}
Correct:
function firstKey(s: string): string | undefined {
  const pairs = JSON.parse(s) as [string, unknown][];
  return pairs[0]?.[0];
}
Complexity: O(n)

