TypeScript · json · edge-cases · L1
Concept: JSON.parse throws SyntaxError on invalid text; wrap it and return a result
Misconception: JSON.parse returns null or undefined for text that is not JSON.
Explanation: Invalid JSON, including single quotes, trailing commas and an empty string, throws SyntaxError. Wrap the call in try/catch and return a discriminated result so callers must handle failure. Parsing user input without a guard crashes the request.
Incorrect:
function parseBad(s: string): unknown {
  return JSON.parse(s);
}
Correct:
type Parsed = { ok: true; value: unknown } | { ok: false; error: string };
function safeParse(s: string): Parsed {
  try {
    return { ok: true, value: JSON.parse(s) };
  } catch (e) {
    return { ok: false, error: (e as Error).message };
  }
}
Complexity: O(n)

