TypeScript · json · access · L0
Concept: JSON.parse returns any, which switches type checking off; parse into unknown and validate
Misconception: JSON.parse(text) returns the type I declare, so p.age + 1 is checked.
Explanation: JSON.parse is typed to return any, so the compiler accepts whatever you do with the result. A missing field gives NaN or a TypeError at run time. Assign the result to unknown, check its shape with a type guard, and only then use it. A cast (as User) does not check anything.
Incorrect:
function nextAgeBad(s: string): number {
  return JSON.parse(s).age + 1;
}
Correct:
function isAge(x: unknown): x is { age: number } {
  if (typeof x !== "object" || x === null) return false;
  return typeof (x as { age?: unknown }).age === "number";
}
function nextAge(s: string): number | null {
  const v: unknown = JSON.parse(s);
  return isAge(v) ? v.age + 1 : null;
}
Complexity: O(n)

