TypeScript · linkedlist · creation · L0
Concept: Build a list by walking the array backwards, or prepending in forward order reverses it
Misconception: Prepending each element while looping forward keeps the original order.
Explanation: A singly linked list grows at the head in O(1), so each prepend puts the new value before all earlier ones. Looping forward therefore produces the reverse order. Walk the input from the end, or keep a tail pointer when appending. A ListNode<T> holds a value and a next reference that is null at the end.
Incorrect:
type ListNode<T> = { value: T; next: ListNode<T> | null };
function fromArrayBad<T>(xs: readonly T[]): ListNode<T> | null {
  let head: ListNode<T> | null = null;
  for (const value of xs) head = { value, next: head };
  return head;
}
Correct:
type ListNode<T> = { value: T; next: ListNode<T> | null };
function fromArray<T>(xs: readonly T[]): ListNode<T> | null {
  let head: ListNode<T> | null = null;
  for (let i = xs.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) head = { value: xs[i] as T, next: head };
  return head;
}
function toArray<T>(head: ListNode<T> | null): T[] {
  const out: T[] = [];
  for (let n = head; n; n = n.next) out.push(n.value);
  return out;
}
Complexity: O(n)

