TypeScript · map · access · L0
Concept: Map.get returns V | undefined, so the compiler makes you handle the missing key before arithmetic
Misconception: map.get(key) always returns the stored type, so m.get(k) + 1 is safe.
Explanation: Unlike an object index signature, Map.get is typed V | undefined. Adding to it is error TS2532 ("Object is possibly undefined"). Supply a default with ?? or check has() first. A non-null assertion (!) silences the error but returns NaN at runtime for a missing key.
Incorrect (compile error TS2532):
function incBad(m: Map<string, number>, k: string): void {
  m.set(k, m.get(k) + 1);
}
Correct:
function inc(m: Map<string, number>, k: string): void {
  m.set(k, (m.get(k) ?? 0) + 1);
}
Complexity: O(1)

