TypeScript · map · ordering · L1
Concept: Map iterates in insertion order; set on an existing key keeps its position, delete then set moves it to the end
Misconception: Calling set on an existing key moves that entry to the end of the iteration order.
Explanation: Updating a key keeps its original position. Only delete followed by set moves it to the end. This is the property an LRU cache relies on: the first key is the oldest, the last key is the newest.
Incorrect:
function touchBad(m: Map<string, number>, k: string, v: number): void {
  m.set(k, v);
}
Correct:
function touch(m: Map<string, number>, k: string, v: number): void {
  m.delete(k);
  m.set(k, v);
}
Complexity: O(1)

