TypeScript · map · equality · L2
Concept: Map and Set treat NaN as equal to NaN and +0 as equal to -0; indexOf does not find NaN
Misconception: NaN is never equal to anything, so it cannot be looked up as a key or found in a list.
Explanation: The === operator says NaN !== NaN, and indexOf uses ===, so indexOf(NaN) is -1. Map, Set and includes use SameValueZero, which treats NaN as equal to itself and +0 as equal to -0. Use includes or a Set when NaN can appear.
Incorrect:
function hasNanBad(xs: readonly number[]): boolean {
  return xs.indexOf(NaN) >= 0;
}
Correct:
function hasNan(xs: readonly number[]): boolean {
  return new Set(xs).has(NaN);
}
Complexity: O(n)

