TypeScript · object · equality · L1
Concept: A type does not forbid extra properties: only fresh object literals are checked, so extra fields can leak through variables
Misconception: A parameter typed { x: number } guarantees that the object has only x.
Explanation: TypeScript is structural: any object with at least the declared properties fits. Excess property errors (TS2353) apply only to object literals written directly at the call. The same object passed through a variable carries extra fields along, and JSON.stringify or spread then exposes them. Copy the fields you want explicitly before sending data out.
Incorrect:
function toJsonBad(p: { x: number }): string {
  return JSON.stringify(p);
}
Correct:
function toJson(p: { x: number }): string {
  return JSON.stringify({ x: p.x });
}
Complexity: O(1)

