TypeScript · object · ordering · L2
Concept: Integer-like keys come first in ascending order, then string keys in insertion order
Misconception: Object.keys returns keys in the order they were added.
Explanation: For ordinary objects the engine orders integer-like keys (such as "1" or "42") numerically before all other keys, whatever the insertion order; other string keys follow in insertion order, then symbols. Code that relies on insertion order for numeric ids breaks. A Map always iterates in insertion order.
Incorrect:
function firstKeyBad(ids: number[]): string | undefined {
  const o: Record<number, boolean> = {};
  for (const id of ids) o[id] = true;
  return Object.keys(o)[0];
}
Correct:
function firstKey(ids: readonly number[]): number | undefined {
  const m = new Map<number, boolean>();
  for (const id of ids) m.set(id, true);
  return m.keys().next().value;
}
Complexity: O(n)

