TypeScript · object · iteration · L1
Concept: Object.keys returns string[], not (keyof T)[]; indexing with those keys fails to compile unless you narrow deliberately
Misconception: for (const k of Object.keys(o)) o[k] type-checks, because k is a key of o.
Explanation: An object of type T can have more properties than T declares, so TypeScript types Object.keys(o) as string[] and rejects o[k] with error TS7053. When you know the object is exact, cast once in a helper: (Object.keys(o) as (keyof T)[]). Object.entries(o) avoids indexing altogether.
Incorrect (compile error TS7053):
function sumBad(o: { a: number; b: number }): number {
  let total = 0;
  for (const k of Object.keys(o)) total += o[k];
  return total;
}
Correct:
function typedKeys<T extends object>(o: T): (keyof T)[] {
  return Object.keys(o) as (keyof T)[];
}
function sum(o: { a: number; b: number }): number {
  let total = 0;
  for (const k of typedKeys(o)) total += o[k];
  return total;
}
Complexity: O(n)

