TypeScript · object · mutation · L2
Concept: readonly is erased at runtime; Object.freeze is the runtime guard and is shallow
Misconception: A property marked readonly cannot be changed at runtime.
Explanation: readonly and Readonly<T> exist only in the type checker. A cast, an alias with a looser type, or plain JavaScript can still change the value. Object.freeze makes the object immutable at runtime (strict-mode writes throw TypeError), but only for its own properties: nested objects stay writable. Freeze nested levels too, or use a copy-on-write style.
Incorrect:
function bumpBad(o: Readonly<{ n: number }>): void {
  (o as { n: number }).n += 1;
}
Correct:
function lock<T extends object>(o: T): Readonly<T> {
  return Object.freeze(o);
}
Complexity: O(1)

