TypeScript · object · mutation · L1
Concept: Object.assign mutates its first argument; spread builds a new object, and the last spread wins
Misconception: Object.assign(defaults, overrides) returns a merged copy and leaves defaults alone.
Explanation: Object.assign copies properties onto its first argument and returns that same object, so a shared defaults object is changed for every later caller. { ...defaults, ...overrides } creates a new object; for duplicate keys the later source wins. Both copy only one level: nested objects stay shared.
Incorrect:
function mergeBad<T extends object>(defaults: T, overrides: Partial<T>): T {
  return Object.assign(defaults, overrides);
}
Correct:
function merge<T extends object>(defaults: T, overrides: Partial<T>): T {
  return { ...defaults, ...overrides };
}
Complexity: O(n)

