TypeScript · priorityqueue · patterns · L2
Concept: Top k largest uses a min-heap of size k; a max-heap discards the wrong end
Misconception: Keeping a bounded heap with a max-first comparator keeps the largest values.
Explanation: To keep the k largest values, evict the smallest of the kept set, which requires a min-heap whose root is the smallest kept item. With a max-heap the eviction removes the largest, so the result is the k smallest. The bounded min-heap costs O(n log k).
Incorrect:
function topKBad(xs: number[], k: number): number[] {
  const q = new PriorityQueue<number>((a, b) => b - a);
  for (const x of xs) {
    q.push(x);
    if (q.size > k) q.pop();
  }
  const out: number[] = [];
  for (let v = q.pop(); v !== undefined; v = q.pop()) out.push(v);
  return out.reverse();
}
Correct:
function topK(xs: number[], k: number): number[] {
  const q = new PriorityQueue<number>((a, b) => a - b);
  for (const x of xs) {
    q.push(x);
    if (q.size > k) q.pop();
  }
  const out: number[] = [];
  for (let v = q.pop(); v !== undefined; v = q.pop()) out.push(v);
  return out.reverse();
}
Complexity: O(n log k)

