TypeScript · priorityqueue · mutation · L3
Concept: Changing an item priority in place does not reorder the heap; push a new entry and skip stale ones
Misconception: Updating an object priority field moves it to the right place in the queue.
Explanation: The heap does not watch its elements. Mutating the field leaves the item where it was, so pop returns the wrong item. The common fix is lazy deletion: push a new entry with the new priority, remember the best priority per key, and discard popped entries that are out of date. Dijkstra with a binary heap uses exactly this.
Incorrect:
type Item = { p: number };
function popAfterMutateBad(): number | undefined {
  const a: Item = { p: 5 };
  const b: Item = { p: 3 };
  const q = new PriorityQueue<Item>((x, y) => x.p - y.p);
  q.push(a);
  q.push(b);
  a.p = 1;
  return q.pop()?.p;
