TypeScript · queue · mutation · L2
Concept: Without compaction an index-based queue never frees consumed slots
Misconception: Advancing the head index frees memory for consumed items.
Explanation: Consumed slots stay in the array, so a long-running queue grows without bound even when it holds one element. Compact the array when the head passes half the length (slice from head and reset to 0); the cost is amortised O(1) per operation.
Incorrect:
class QueueBad<T> {
  private items: T[] = [];
  private head = 0;
  enqueue(x: T): void { this.items.push(x); }
  dequeue(): T | undefined { return this.items[this.head++]; }
  get size(): number { return this.items.length; }
  storage(): number { return this.items.length; }
}
Correct:
class Queue<T> {
  private items: T[] = [];
  private head = 0;
  enqueue(x: T): void { this.items.push(x); }
  dequeue(): T | undefined {
    if (this.head >= this.items.length) return undefined;
    const x = this.items[this.head++] as T;
    if (this.head * 2 >= this.items.length) {
      this.items = this.items.slice(this.head);
      this.head = 0;
    }
    return x;
  }
  peek(): T | undefined { return this.items[this.head]; }
  get size(): number { return this.items.length - this.head; }
  storage(): number { return this.items.length; }
}
Complexity: O(1) amortised

