TypeScript · queue · patterns · L3
Concept: A queue from two stacks moves items only when the output stack is empty
Misconception: Moving all items to the output stack on every dequeue keeps FIFO order.
Explanation: Items are pushed on an input stack and popped from an output stack. Transferring reverses their order once. If you transfer on every dequeue, items already in the output stack end up beneath newer ones, so the order breaks. Transfer only when the output stack is empty; each element moves at most once, giving amortised O(1).
Incorrect:
class TwoStackBad<T> {
  private inp: T[] = [];
  private out: T[] = [];
  enqueue(x: T): void { this.inp.push(x); }
  dequeue(): T | undefined {
    while (this.inp.length) this.out.push(this.inp.pop() as T);
    return this.out.pop();
  }
}
Correct:
class TwoStackQueue<T> {
  private inp: T[] = [];
  private out: T[] = [];
  enqueue(x: T): void { this.inp.push(x); }
  dequeue(): T | undefined {
    if (!this.out.length) while (this.inp.length) this.out.push(this.inp.pop() as T);
    return this.out.pop();
  }
}
Complexity: O(1) amortised

