TypeScript · regexp · patterns · L2
Concept: Quantifiers are greedy: .+ takes as much as possible; use .+? or a negated class to stop early
Misconception: /<.+>/g finds each HTML tag separately.
Explanation: A greedy .+ consumes up to the last > on the line, so two adjacent tags become one match. Use the lazy form .+? or a negated class [^>]+ to stop at the first >. Nested quantifiers such as (a+)+ can also backtrack catastrophically; prefer simple, anchored patterns for untrusted input.
Incorrect:
function tagsBad(html: string): string[] {
  return html.match(/<.+>/g) ?? [];
}
Correct:
function tags(html: string): string[] {
  return html.match(/<[^>]+>/g) ?? [];
}
Complexity: O(n)

