Concept: Without the u flag, . and \w work on UTF-16 units; emoji count as two characters
Misconception: /./g counts one match per visible character.
Explanation: JavaScript strings are UTF-16. A character outside the basic plane, such as an emoji, is two code units, and . matches each half separately. The u flag makes . match a whole code point. Use /\p{L}/u for letters in any language. Even with u, a combined emoji sequence can contain several code points.
Incorrect:
function lenBad(s: string): number {
  return s.match(/./g)?.length ?? 0;
}
Correct:
function len(s: string): number {
  return s.match(/./gu)?.length ?? 0;
}
Complexity: O(n)

