TypeScript · segmenttree · creation · L1
Concept: A recursive segment tree needs an array of 4n slots; 2n is not enough when n is not a power of two
Misconception: A segment tree over n items fits in 2n slots.
Explanation: A recursive tree with children at 2*node and 2*node+1 leaves gaps when n is not a power of two, so node numbers can pass 2n. For n = 6 the highest node used is 13, which is past 12. Allocating 4n is always enough. An iterative bottom-up tree can use exactly 2n because it uses a different layout.
Incorrect:
function sizeBad(n: number): number {
  return 2 * n;
}
Correct:
function treeSize(n: number): number {
  return 4 * n;
}
Complexity: O(n) space

