TypeScript · segmenttree · mutation · L2
Concept: A point update must recompute every ancestor on the way back up
Misconception: Writing the new value in the leaf is enough.
Explanation: Ancestors store combined values. After changing a leaf, each node on the path to the root must be recomputed from its children, which the recursion does after returning. Updating the leaf only leaves stale sums in every ancestor, so any query that uses an ancestor is wrong.
Incorrect:
function updateBad(
  t: SegTree, i: number, v: number, node = 1, l = 0, r = t.n - 1,
): void {
  if (l === r) { t.tree[node] = v; return; }
  const mid = (l + r) >> 1;
  if (i <= mid) updateBad(t, i, v, 2 * node, l, mid);
  else updateBad(t, i, v, 2 * node + 1, mid + 1, r);
}
Correct:
function setPoint(
  t: SegTree, i: number, v: number, node = 1, l = 0, r = t.n - 1,
): void {
  if (l === r) { t.tree[node] = v; return; }
  const mid = (l + r) >> 1;
  if (i <= mid) setPoint(t, i, v, 2 * node, l, mid);
  else setPoint(t, i, v, 2 * node + 1, mid + 1, r);
  t.tree[node] = (t.tree[2 * node] as number) + (t.tree[2 * node + 1] as number);
}
Complexity: O(log n)

