TypeScript · set · transformation · L2
Concept: union, intersection and difference return new Sets; hand-written loops often mutate an input
Misconception: Removing items from a while iterating is a safe way to compute the intersection with b.
Explanation: Deleting during iteration is legal, but it changes the input set, which surprises callers. The ES2025 methods a.union(b), a.intersection(b), a.difference(b) and a.symmetricDifference(b) return new Sets and leave both inputs alone. Check the runtime supports them (Node 22 does).
Incorrect:
function commonBad(a: Set<number>, b: ReadonlySet<number>): Set<number> {
  for (const x of a) if (!b.has(x)) a.delete(x);
  return a;
}
Correct:
function common(a: ReadonlySet<number>, b: ReadonlySet<number>): Set<number> {
  return a.intersection(b);
}
Complexity: O(min(n, m))

