TypeScript · set · fit-and-alternatives · L3
Concept: Set<"a" | "b">.has(x) rejects a plain string; widen the Set to ReadonlySet<string> to use has as a type guard
Misconception: ALLOWED.has(input) narrows input to the literal union.
Explanation: Set.has is typed to accept only T, so a string argument is error TS2345 when T is a literal union, and has does not narrow its argument anyway. Declare the Set as ReadonlySet<string>, then write an explicit type guard that returns input is Role.
Incorrect (compile error TS2345):
const ROLES = new Set(["admin", "user"] as const);
type Role = "admin" | "user";
function isRoleBad(x: string): x is Role {
  return ROLES.has(x);
}
Correct:
const ROLE_SET: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set(["admin", "user"]);
type Role = "admin" | "user";
function isRole(x: string): x is Role {
  return ROLE_SET.has(x);
}
Complexity: O(1)

