TypeScript · set · mutation · L3
Concept: Return ReadonlySet<T> to stop callers adding values; the type is erased, so copy when you need real protection
Misconception: A function that returns its internal Set can be trusted not to have it modified by callers.
Explanation: A returned Set<T> lets callers call add and delete on your private state. The type ReadonlySet<T> removes those methods at compile time (TS2339 on add). The restriction is not enforced at runtime, so return new Set(internal) when callers are untrusted.
Incorrect (compile error TS2339):
function addBad(s: ReadonlySet<number>): void {
  s.add(1);
}
Correct:
class Tags {
  private items = new Set<string>();
  add(t: string): void { this.items.add(t); }
  view(): ReadonlySet<string> { return this.items; }
  copy(): Set<string> { return new Set(this.items); }
}
Complexity: O(1)

