TypeScript · sharedarraybuffer · mutation · L2
Concept: Use Atomics.add for a counter shared between threads; i[0]++ is a separate read and write that can lose updates
Misconception: i[0]++ on a shared Int32Array is safe because it is one statement.
Explanation: A plain increment reads the value, adds one and writes it back. Two threads can read the same value and both write it plus one, so an update is lost. Atomics.add performs the whole operation indivisibly and returns the previous value. The plain version is wrong only sometimes, which is what makes it dangerous.
Incorrect:
function incrementBad(i: Int32Array, n: number): void {
  for (let k = 0; k < n; k++) i[0]++;
}
Correct:
function increment(i: Int32Array, n: number): void {
  for (let k = 0; k < n; k++) Atomics.add(i, 0, 1);
}
Complexity: O(n)

