TypeScript · sharedarraybuffer · testing · L3
Concept: Atomics.wait returns "ok", "not-equal" or "timed-out"; only "ok" means another thread woke you
Misconception: If Atomics.wait returns normally, another thread called notify.
Explanation: Atomics.wait blocks until notified, until the timeout, or immediately when the value is not the expected one. It returns "ok", "timed-out" or "not-equal". Treating anything other than "not-equal" as success reports a timeout as a notification. Browsers forbid wait on the main thread; Node allows it.
Incorrect:
function notifiedBad(i: Int32Array, ms: number): boolean {
  return Atomics.wait(i, 0, 0, ms) !== "not-equal";
}
Correct:
function notified(i: Int32Array, ms: number): boolean {
  return Atomics.wait(i, 0, 0, ms) === "ok";
}
Complexity: O(1)

