document:
TypeScript · stack · patterns · L3
Concept: A min stack keeps a parallel stack of minimums; a single min variable is wrong after a pop
Misconception: Storing one running minimum is enough to answer getMin after pops.
Explanation: When the minimum element is popped, the previous minimum is lost if only one variable is kept. Keep a second stack that records the minimum at every depth, or store pairs (value, minSoFar). Both give O(1) push, pop and getMin.
Incorrect:
class MinStackBad {
  private items: number[] = [];
  private min = Infinity;
  push(x: number): void { this.items.push(x); this.min = Math.min(this.min, x); }
  pop(): number | undefined { return this.items.pop(); }
  getMin(): number { return this.min; }
}
Correct:
class MinStack {
  private items: number[] = [];
  private mins: number[] = [];
  push(x: number): void {
    this.items.push(x);
    this.mins.push(Math.min(x, this.mins[this.mins.length - 1] ?? Infinity));
  }
  pop(): number | undefined {
    this.mins.pop();
    return this.items.pop();
  }
  getMin(): number { return this.mins[this.mins.length - 1] ?? Infinity; }
}
Complexity: O(1)

