document:
TypeScript · stack · patterns · L3
Concept: A monotonic stack finds the next greater element; use while, not if, when popping smaller values
Misconception: Popping one smaller value per new element is enough.
Explanation: A new value can be greater than several stack entries, and each of them has just found its answer. Using if pops only one, leaving unresolved entries behind and producing wrong answers. The while loop pops every smaller value, so each index is pushed and popped once: O(n).
Incorrect:
function nextGreaterBad(xs: number[]): number[] {
  const res = xs.map(() => -1);
  const st: number[] = [];
  xs.forEach((x, i) => {
    const t = st[st.length - 1];
    if (t !== undefined && (xs[t] as number) < x) res[st.pop() as number] = x;
    st.push(i);
  });
  return res;
}
Correct:
function nextGreater(xs: number[]): number[] {
  const res = xs.map(() => -1);
  const st: number[] = [];
  xs.forEach((x, i) => {
    while (st.length && (xs[st[st.length - 1] as number] as number) < x) {
      res[st.pop() as number] = x;
    }
    st.push(i);
  });
  return res;
}
Complexity: O(n)

