TypeScript · trie · access · L1
Concept: Whole-word search checks the end flag; reaching the node only proves a prefix exists
Misconception: If walking the word ends at a node, the word is in the trie.
Explanation: A node exists for every prefix of every stored word. After inserting "apple", the path for "app" exists although "app" was never inserted. A word search must also check that the final node is marked as the end of a word, while startsWith only needs the path.
Incorrect:
function hasBad(t: Trie, word: string): boolean {
  return t.find(word) !== undefined;
}
Correct:
function hasWord(t: Trie, word: string): boolean {
  const node = t.find(word);
  return node !== undefined && node.end;
}
Complexity: O(L)

