TypeScript · typedarray · equality · L2
Concept: Typed arrays are compared by content with a loop; === and == compare identity
Misconception: a === b is true for typed arrays holding the same values.
Explanation: A typed array is an object, so === compares identity. Compare lengths, then elements. Array.prototype.every can be called on typed arrays directly. NaN never equals itself, so use Object.is when floats may be NaN.
Incorrect:
function sameBad(a: Uint8Array, b: Uint8Array): boolean {
  return a === b;
}
Correct:
function same(a: Uint8Array, b: Uint8Array): boolean {
  return a.length === b.length && a.every((v, i) => v === b[i]);
}
Complexity: O(n)

