TypeScript · typedarray · ordering · L1
Concept: TypedArray.sort compares numerically by default, while Array.sort compares strings
Misconception: Array.sort() and TypedArray.sort() order numbers the same way.
Explanation: Array.prototype.sort without a comparator converts elements to strings, so [10, 9, 1] becomes [1, 10, 9]. TypedArray.prototype.sort compares numerically, giving [1, 9, 10]. A typed array is also a quick way to sort numbers without writing a comparator. toSorted returns a sorted copy.
Incorrect:
function sortBad(xs: readonly number[]): number[] {
  return [...xs].sort();
}
Correct:
function sortNums(xs: readonly number[]): number[] {
  return [...Float64Array.from(xs).sort()];
}
Complexity: O(n log n)

