document:
TypeScript · undirectedgraph · mutation · L1
Concept: An undirected edge is stored in both neighbour sets, and a Set ignores duplicates
Misconception: Adding v to the neighbours of u is enough.
Explanation: An undirected edge connects u and v both ways. Storing it once makes hasEdge(v, u) false and traversals from v miss the edge. Add both ends. Using a Set per vertex also makes repeated addEdge calls harmless.
Incorrect:
function addOneWayBad(g: UGraph, u: number, v: number): void {
  (g.adj[u] as Set<number>).add(v);
}
Correct:
class UGraph {
  adj: Array<Set<number>>;
  constructor(readonly n: number) {
    this.adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => new Set<number>());
  }
  addEdge(u: number, v: number): void {
    (this.adj[u] as Set<number>).add(v);
    (this.adj[v] as Set<number>).add(u);
  }
  hasEdge(u: number, v: number): boolean {
    return (this.adj[u] as Set<number>).has(v);
  }
}
Complexity: O(1)

