TypeScript · weakmap · access · L0
Concept: WeakMap keys must be objects (or non-registered symbols); a string key is error TS2345
Misconception: A WeakMap works like a Map, so a string can be used as a key.
Explanation: WeakMap<K, V> requires K to be a WeakKey: an object, function or non-registered symbol. A primitive key is rejected by the compiler (TS2345) and throws TypeError at runtime, because primitives cannot be garbage collected independently. Use a Map when keys are strings or numbers.
Incorrect (compile error TS2345):
function tagBad(wm: WeakMap<object, number>): void {
  wm.set("a", 1);
}
Correct:
function tag(wm: WeakMap<object, number>, o: object, n: number): void {
  wm.set(o, n);
}
Complexity: O(1)

