document:
TypeScript · weakmap · fit-and-alternatives · L2
Concept: Cache results per object in a WeakMap so cached entries do not keep the objects alive
Misconception: A Map keyed by object is an equivalent cache.
Explanation: A Map holds strong references to its keys, so every object ever cached stays in memory for the life of the cache. A WeakMap holds keys weakly: when the object is otherwise unreachable, its entry can be collected. The trade-off is that a WeakMap cannot be listed, counted or cleared.
Incorrect:
function memoBad<T extends object, R>(fn: (x: T) => R): ((x: T) => R) & { size(): number } {
  const cache = new Map<T, R>();
  const f = (x: T): R => {
    if (!cache.has(x)) cache.set(x, fn(x));
    return cache.get(x) as R;
  };
  return Object.assign(f, { size: () => cache.size });
}
Correct:
function memo<T extends object, R>(fn: (x: T) => R): (x: T) => R {
  const cache = new WeakMap<T, R>();
  return (x: T): R => {
    if (!cache.has(x)) cache.set(x, fn(x));
    return cache.get(x) as R;
  };
}
Complexity: O(1)

