2. Overview and mental model: Heap
A binary heap is a complete binary tree stored in an array. In a min-heap every parent is not larger than its children,
so the minimum is at index 0. A max-heap flips the comparison; one common trick is to store negated numbers in a
min-heap.
With 0-based indexes, the parent of i is (i - 1) >> 1 and its children are 2i + 1 and 2i + 2. Push sifts the new value up;
pop moves the last value to the root and sifts it down; heapify builds the heap in O(n).
The same structure powers priority queues, heap sort, top-k selection and streaming medians. In TypeScript,
remember that array reads can be undefined under noUncheckedIndexedAccess, which is why the code here casts
reads that are proven in range.
Toolbox
 const h = new MinHeap();
h.push(5); h.push(1); h.push(3);
h.peek();                    // 1
h.pop();                     // 1
const parent = (i: number) => (i - 1) >> 1;
const heap = heapify([5, 4, 3, 2, 1]);

TypeScript Heap: Training Data for the DSA Mentor RAG Chatbot
Page 3
3. Core concepts (tested chunks)
3 concept chunks, ordered by level. Read the misconception first and try to predict why it fails before reading the
explanation.
In a 0-indexed array heap the parent of i is (i - 1) >> 1 and the children are 2i + 1 and 2i + 2
ts-heap-index-math   lens: access   level: L1   family: ts-heap-access
Prerequisites: none. Follow-up: ts-heap-sift-up. Complexity: O(1).
 Common misconception. The parent of index i is floor(i / 2) and the children are 2i and 2i + 1.
Those formulas are for a 1-indexed heap that leaves slot 0 empty. A 0-indexed array uses parent = (i - 1) >> 1, left =
2i + 1, right = 2i + 2. With the 1-indexed formulas, index 2 gets parent 1 instead of 0 and the heap property is checked
against the wrong node.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 function parentBad(i: number): number {
  return Math.floor(i / 2);
}
Correct:
 function parent(i: number): number {
  return (i - 1) >> 1;
}
function children(i: number): [number, number] {
  return [2 * i + 1, 2 * i + 2];
}
Sift-down swaps with the smaller child; always taking the left child breaks the heap
ts-heap-sift-down   lens: insertion-and-deletion   level: L2   family: ts-heap-delete
Prerequisites: ts-heap-sift-up. Follow-up: ts-heap-heapify. Complexity: O(log n).
 Common misconception. After moving the last element to the root, swap it with the left child until it is smaller.
The root must end up smaller than both children, so it should swap with the smaller one. Taking the left child
unconditionally can put a larger value above a smaller sibling. Compare both children, pick the smaller (for a
min-heap), and stop when neither is smaller than the node.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 function siftDownLeft(a: number[], start: number, n: number): void {
  let i = start;
  for (;;) {
    const l = 2 * i + 1;
    if (l >= n || (a[l] as number) >= (a[i] as number)) return;
    [a[i], a[l]] = [a[l] as number, a[i] as number];
    i = l;
  }
}

