levels.
 Answers live only in the exercise payload. The embedded text of an exercise is the prompt alone, so retrieval
cannot leak a solution into a hint.
2. Overview and mental model: Segment Tree
A segment tree answers range queries (sum, min, max) and supports updates, each in O(log n), by storing the
combined value of every range in a binary tree over the array.
The recursive layout puts the children of node i at 2i and 2i+1 and needs 4n slots. The iterative bottom-up layout uses
exactly 2n slots and half-open ranges. Lazy propagation postpones range updates by storing tags on nodes.
Use it when the operation has no inverse (min, max, gcd) or when range updates and range queries are mixed. For
plain sums a Fenwick tree is shorter.
Toolbox
 const t = new SegTree([5, 3, 8, 1, 9]);
sumRange(t, 1, 3);       // 12
setPoint(t, 2, 0);       // update and recompute ancestors
sumRange(t, 1, 3);       // 4
3. Core concepts (tested chunks)
2 concept chunks, ordered by level. Read the misconception first and try to predict why it fails before reading the
explanation.

TypeScript Segment Tree: Training Data for the DSA Mentor RAG Chatbot
Page 3
A recursive segment tree needs an array of 4n slots; 2n is not enough when n is not a power
of two
ts-segmenttree-size   lens: creation   level: L1   family: ts-seg-creation
Prerequisites: none. Follow-up: ts-segmenttree-build. Complexity: O(n) space.
 Common misconception. A segment tree over n items fits in 2n slots.
A recursive tree with children at 2*node and 2*node+1 leaves gaps when n is not a power of two, so node numbers
can pass 2n. For n = 6 the highest node used is 13, which is past 12. Allocating 4n is always enough. An iterative
bottom-up tree can use exactly 2n because it uses a different layout.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 function sizeBad(n: number): number {
  return 2 * n;
}
Correct:
 function treeSize(n: number): number {
  return 4 * n;
}
Build combines both children into every internal node, not just one
ts-segmenttree-build   lens: creation   level: L2   family: ts-seg-creation
Prerequisites: ts-segmenttree-size. Follow-up: ts-segmenttree-query. Complexity: O(n).
 Common misconception. An internal node can copy the value of its left child.
Each internal node stores the combined value of its range, which is the left child plus the right child for a sum.
Copying only one child makes every internal node ignore half of its range, so queries answered from internal nodes
are wrong.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 function buildBad(a: number[], tree: number[], node: number, l: number, r: number): void {
  if (l === r) { tree[node] = a[l] as number; return; }
  const mid = (l + r) >> 1;
  buildBad(a, tree, 2 * node, l, mid);
  buildBad(a, tree, 2 * node + 1, mid + 1, r);
  tree[node] = tree[2 * node] as number;
}
Correct:
 class SegTree {
  n: number;
  tree: number[];
  constructor(a: number[]) {
    this.n = a.length;
    this.tree = new Array<number>(4 * Math.max(1, this.n)).fill(0);
    if (this.n > 0) this.build(a, 1, 0, this.n - 1);
  }
  build(a: number[], node: number, l: number, r: number): void {
    if (l === r) {
      this.tree[node] = a[l] as number;
      return;
    }
    const mid = (l + r) >> 1;
    this.build(a, 2 * node, l, mid);
    this.build(a, 2 * node + 1, mid + 1, r);
    this.tree[node] = (this.tree[2 * node] as number) + (this.tree[2 * node + 1] as number);
  }
}

