Build the adjacency list with Array.from; fill([]) shares one inner array between all vertices
ts-directedgraph-creation   lens: creation   level: L1   family: ts-dg-creation
Prerequisites: none. Follow-up: ts-directedgraph-direction. Complexity: O(V).
 Common misconception. new Array(n).fill([]) creates n empty neighbour lists.
fill puts the same array object in every slot. Adding a neighbour to one vertex adds it to all of them. Create a separate
array for each vertex with Array.from({ length: n }, () => []), which calls the factory once per slot.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 function makeBad(n: number): number[][] {
  return new Array(n).fill([]) as number[][];
}
Correct:
 class DiGraph {
  adj: number[][];
  constructor(readonly n: number) {
    this.adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => [] as number[]);
  }
  addEdge(u: number, v: number): void {
    (this.adj[u] as number[]).push(v);
  }
}
A directed edge is stored once, on the source vertex only
ts-directedgraph-direction   lens: mutation   level: L1   family: ts-dg-mutation
Prerequisites: ts-directedgraph-creation. Follow-up: ts-directedgraph-cycle. Complexity: O(1).
 Common misconception. addEdge(u, v) should also record v to u.
In a directed graph the edge u to v does not imply v to u. Adding both directions turns the graph into an undirected
one: cycle detection, topological order and strongly connected components all give wrong answers. Store the edge
only in adj[u].
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 function addEdgeBad(g: DiGraph, u: number, v: number): void {
  (g.adj[u] as number[]).push(v);
  (g.adj[v] as number[]).push(u);
}
Correct:
 function addEdgeOnce(g: DiGraph, u: number, v: number): void {
  (g.adj[u] as number[]).push(v);
}

