Shortest hop count needs BFS; the first path found by DFS can be much longer
ts-directedgraph-bfs   lens: patterns   level: L2   family: ts-dg-patterns
Prerequisites: ts-directedgraph-topo. Follow-up: ts-directedgraph-scc. Complexity: O(V + E).
 Common misconception. Any traversal that sets dist when it first reaches a node gives the shortest distance.
BFS reaches nodes in order of distance, so the first time a node is reached is the shortest. DFS follows one branch to
the end, so the first time it reaches a node may be through a long path. Use a queue for unweighted shortest paths.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 function dfsDistBad(g: DiGraph, src: number): number[] {
  const dist = new Array<number>(g.n).fill(-1);
  const go = (u: number, d: number): void => {
    dist[u] = d;
    for (const v of g.adj[u] as number[]) if (dist[v] === -1) go(v, d + 1);
  };
  go(src, 0);
  return dist;
}
Correct:
 function bfsDist(g: DiGraph, src: number): number[] {
  const dist = new Array<number>(g.n).fill(-1);
  dist[src] = 0;
  const queue = [src];
  for (let i = 0; i < queue.length; i++) {
    const u = queue[i] as number;
    for (const v of g.adj[u] as number[]) {
      if (dist[v] === -1) {
        dist[v] = (dist[u] as number) + 1;
        queue.push(v);
      }
    }
  }
  return dist;
}
A DFS finishing order must be reversed to be a topological order; Kahn removes sources
ts-directedgraph-topo   lens: ordering   level: L3   family: ts-dg-ordering
Prerequisites: ts-directedgraph-cycle. Follow-up: ts-directedgraph-bfs. Complexity: O(V + E).
 Common misconception. The order in which DFS finishes nodes is a topological order.
DFS finishes a node after all of its descendants, so the finishing order lists dependencies first, which is the reverse of
what a schedule needs. Reverse it, or use Kahn's algorithm: repeatedly take a vertex with indegree zero. If fewer
than n vertices come out, the graph has a cycle.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 function topoBad(g: DiGraph): number[] {
  const seen = new Array<boolean>(g.n).fill(false);
  const out: number[] = [];
  const visit = (u: number): void => {
    seen[u] = true;
    for (const v of g.adj[u] as number[]) if (!seen[v]) visit(v);
    out.push(u);
  };
  for (let u = 0; u < g.n; u++) if (!seen[u]) visit(u);
  return out;
}

TypeScript Directed Graph: Training Data for the DSA Mentor RAG Chatbot
Page 6
Correct:
 function topoSort(g: DiGraph): number[] {
  const indeg = new Array<number>(g.n).fill(0);
  for (const row of g.adj) for (const v of row) indeg[v] = (indeg[v] as number) + 1;
  const queue: number[] = [];
  indeg.forEach((d, u) => { if (d === 0) queue.push(u); });
  for (let i = 0; i < queue.length; i++) {
    for (const v of g.adj[queue[i] as number] as number[]) {
      indeg[v] = (indeg[v] as number) - 1;
      if (indeg[v] === 0) queue.push(v);
    }
  }
  return queue.length === g.n ? queue : [];
}
Cycle detection needs three states; a seen flag reports a cycle for any node reached twice
ts-directedgraph-cycle   lens: patterns   level: L3   family: ts-dg-patterns
Prerequisites: ts-directedgraph-direction. Follow-up: ts-directedgraph-topo. Complexity: O(V + E).
 Common misconception. If DFS reaches a visited node again, there is a cycle.
In a DAG a node can be reached by two different paths (a diamond) without any cycle. A cycle exists only when DFS
reaches a node that is still on the current recursion stack. Use three states: unvisited, on the stack, and finished.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 function hasCycleBad(g: DiGraph): boolean {
  const seen = new Array<boolean>(g.n).fill(false);
  const visit = (u: number): boolean => {
    seen[u] = true;
    for (const v of g.adj[u] as number[]) {
      if (seen[v] || visit(v)) return true;
    }
    return false;
  };
  for (let u = 0; u < g.n; u++) if (!seen[u] && visit(u)) return true;
  return false;
}
Correct:
 function hasCycle(g: DiGraph): boolean {
  const state = new Array<number>(g.n).fill(0);
  const visit = (u: number): boolean => {
    state[u] = 1;
    for (const v of g.adj[u] as number[]) {
      if (state[v] === 1) return true;
      if (state[v] === 0 && visit(v)) return true;
    }
    state[u] = 2;
    return false;
  };
  for (let u = 0; u < g.n; u++) if (state[u] === 0 && visit(u)) return true;
  return false;
}

