Prompt. How does a topological sort detect a cycle, and why does it matter for build systems?
Answer key (mentor only, never embedded). Kahn's algorithm removes vertices with indegree zero one at a time. If
the graph has a cycle, the vertices on the cycle never reach indegree zero, so fewer than n vertices are output. A build
system needs a dependency order, so a cycle means no valid build order exists and the tool must report the cycle
instead of looping.
