2. Overview and mental model: Deque
A deque supports push and pop at both ends in O(1). JavaScript arrays are O(1) at the back only; unshift and shift
move every element. A deque keeps a head and a tail index (here in a Map, or in a circular buffer) so both ends are
cheap.
The typing rule is the same as for stacks and queues: pops and peeks return T | undefined, which the compiler forces
you to handle.
Deques solve sliding window problems (monotonic deque), 0-1 BFS, palindromes and work stealing.
Toolbox
 const d = new Deque<number>();
d.pushBack(2); d.pushFront(1); d.pushBack(3);
d.popFront();            // 1
d.popBack();             // 3
d.peekFront()?.toFixed(1);
3. Core concepts (tested chunks)
3 concept chunks, ordered by level. Read the misconception first and try to predict why it fails before reading the
explanation.

TypeScript Deque: Training Data for the DSA Mentor RAG Chatbot
Page 3
A deque grows at both ends: pushFront must decrement the head before writing
ts-deque-core   lens: creation   level: L1   family: ts-deque-creation
Prerequisites: none. Follow-up: ts-deque-empty. Complexity: O(1).
 Common misconception. this.items.set(this.head--, x) inserts at the front.
The head index points at the first element, so a new front element goes one slot before it. Post-decrement writes at
the old head, overwriting the current first element and leaving the new head empty. Pre-decrement (--head) writes at
the new slot. A Map keyed by integer index, or a circular buffer, gives O(1) at both ends; Array.unshift is O(n).
Mnemonist (npm) has FixedDeque.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 class DequeBad<T> {
  private items = new Map<number, T>();
  private head = 0;
  private tail = 0;
  pushBack(x: T): void { this.items.set(this.tail++, x); }
  pushFront(x: T): void { this.items.set(this.head--, x); }
  toArray(): (T | undefined)[] {
    const out: (T | undefined)[] = [];
    for (let i = this.head; i < this.tail; i++) out.push(this.items.get(i));
    return out;
  }
}
Correct:
 class Deque<T> {
  private items = new Map<number, T>();
  private head = 0;
  private tail = 0;
  pushBack(x: T): void { this.items.set(this.tail++, x); }
  pushFront(x: T): void { this.items.set(--this.head, x); }
  popFront(): T | undefined {
    if (this.head === this.tail) return undefined;
    const x = this.items.get(this.head);
    this.items.delete(this.head++);
    return x;
  }
  popBack(): T | undefined {
    if (this.head === this.tail) return undefined;
    const x = this.items.get(--this.tail);
    this.items.delete(this.tail);
    return x;
  }
  peekFront(): T | undefined { return this.items.get(this.head); }
  peekBack(): T | undefined { return this.items.get(this.tail - 1); }
  get size(): number { return this.tail - this.head; }
  toArray(): T[] {
    const out: T[] = [];
    for (let i = this.head; i < this.tail; i++) out.push(this.items.get(i) as T);
    return out;
  }
}

TypeScript Deque: Training Data for the DSA Mentor RAG Chatbot
Page 4
peekFront and peekBack return T | undefined, so using the result directly is error TS2532
ts-deque-peek   lens: access   level: L1   family: ts-deque-access
Prerequisites: ts-deque-empty. Follow-up: ts-deque-rotate. Complexity: O(1).
 Common misconception. The front element of a deque of numbers can be used without a check.
An empty deque has no front, so the return type includes undefined. Calling a method on it is error TS2532. Test size
first, or use optional chaining (d.peekFront()?.toFixed(1)).
Incorrect (rejected by the compiler, TS2532):
 function fixedBad(d: Deque<number>): string {
  return d.peekFront().toFixed(1);
}
Correct:
 function fixed(d: Deque<number>): string | undefined {
  return d.peekFront()?.toFixed(1);
}
Popping an empty deque must not move the indexes, or size goes negative
ts-deque-empty   lens: edge-cases   level: L1   family: ts-deque-edge
Prerequisites: ts-deque-core. Follow-up: ts-deque-peek. Complexity: O(1).
 Common misconception. popBack can decrement tail without checking whether the deque is empty.
On an empty deque head equals tail. Decrementing tail below head makes size negative, and later pushes land in the
wrong slots. Guard every pop with an emptiness test and return undefined.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 class DequeBadPop<T> {
  private items = new Map<number, T>();
  private head = 0;
  private tail = 0;
  pushBack(x: T): void { this.items.set(this.tail++, x); }
  popBack(): T | undefined {
    const x = this.items.get(--this.tail);
    this.items.delete(this.tail);
    return x;
  }
  get size(): number { return this.tail - this.head; }
}

