Map iterates in insertion order; set on an existing key keeps its position, delete then set
moves it to the end
ts-map-insertion-order   lens: ordering   level: L1   family: ts-map-order
Prerequisites: ts-map-nan-zero. Follow-up: ts-map-group-by. Complexity: O(1).
Common misconception. Calling set on an existing key moves that entry to the end of the iteration order.
Updating a key keeps its original position. Only delete followed by set moves it to the end. This is the property an
LRU cache relies on: the first key is the oldest, the last key is the newest.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
function touchBad(m: Map<string, number>, k: string, v: number): void {
m.set(k, v);
}
Correct:
function touch(m: Map<string, number>, k: string, v: number): void {
m.delete(k);
m.set(k, v);
}
Map and Set treat NaN as equal to NaN and +0 as equal to -0; indexOf does not find NaN
ts-map-nan-zero   lens: equality   level: L2   family: ts-map-keys
Prerequisites: ts-map-key-identity. Follow-up: ts-map-insertion-order. Complexity: O(n).
Common misconception. NaN is never equal to anything, so it cannot be looked up as a key or found in a list.
The === operator says NaN !== NaN, and indexOf uses ===, so indexOf(NaN) is -1. Map, Set and includes use
SameValueZero, which treats NaN as equal to itself and +0 as equal to -0. Use includes or a Set when NaN can
appear.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
function hasNanBad(xs: readonly number[]): boolean {
return xs.indexOf(NaN) >= 0;
}
Correct:
function hasNan(xs: readonly number[]): boolean {
return new Set(xs).has(NaN);
}

