Entries added during iteration are visited; iterate over a snapshot when you add keys
ts-map-iterate-mutate   lens: iteration   level: L2   family: ts-map-iteration
Prerequisites: ts-map-group-by. Follow-up: ts-map-vs-object-json. Complexity: O(n).
Common misconception. A for...of loop over a Map sees only the entries that existed when the loop began.
Map iteration is live. Deleting the current entry is safe, but a key added during the loop is visited later, so a loop that
adds a key per entry never ends. Copy first with [...m] when you need to add or reorder.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
function spreadBad(m: Map<number, number>): number {
let visits = 0;
for (const [k, v] of m) {
visits++;
if (visits > 50) break;
m.set(k + 1, v);
}
return visits;
}
Correct:
function spread(m: Map<number, number>): number {
let visits = 0;
for (const [k, v] of [...m]) {
visits++;
m.set(k + 1, v);
}
return visits;
}
Map.groupBy builds Map<K, T[]> in one call; hand-written grouping must create the array
before pushing
ts-map-group-by   lens: transformation   level: L2   family: ts-map-transform
Prerequisites: ts-map-insertion-order. Follow-up: ts-map-iterate-mutate. Complexity: O(n).
Common misconception. m.get(key).push(x) works for every key once the Map is created.
A new Map has no arrays, so get returns undefined for the first item of each group and push throws TypeError.
Create the bucket on first use, or call Map.groupBy(items, fn), which returns a Map of arrays in first-seen key order.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):

TypeScript Map · 4. Grouping, ordering and caching strategies (continued)
function groupBad(xs: readonly number[]): Map<boolean, number[]> {
const m = new Map<boolean, number[]>();
for (const x of xs) m.get(x % 2 === 0)!.push(x);
return m;
}
Correct:
