JSON.stringify(map) gives "{}"; convert with [...map] or Object.fromEntries first
ts-map-vs-object-json   lens: fit-and-alternatives   level: L2   family: ts-map-fit
Prerequisites: ts-map-iterate-mutate. Follow-up: ts-map-lru. Complexity: O(n).
Common misconception. JSON.stringify serialises a Map like any other collection.
A Map has no enumerable own properties, so JSON.stringify returns "{}" and the data is lost silently. Serialise [...map]
(an array of pairs) and rebuild with new Map(pairs). Object.fromEntries works when all keys are strings.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
function saveBad(m: Map<string, number>): string {
return JSON.stringify(m);
}
Correct:
function save(m: Map<string, number>): string {
return JSON.stringify([...m]);
}
function load(s: string): Map<string, number> {
return new Map(JSON.parse(s) as [string, number][]);
}
An LRU cache is a Map that moves a key to the end on every read and evicts the first key
when full
ts-map-lru   lens: patterns   level: L3   family: ts-map-patterns
Prerequisites: ts-map-vs-object-json. Follow-up: none. Complexity: O(1).
Common misconception. Reading an entry from a Map does not change eviction order.
An LRU cache must treat a read as a use. A Map gives O(1) lookup and ordered keys, so a read does delete then set
to mark the entry newest. Eviction removes map.keys().next().value, the oldest. Without the refresh on get, the cache
is FIFO.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):

