An undirected edge is stored in both neighbour sets, and a Set ignores duplicates
ts-undirectedgraph-edge   lens: mutation   level: L1   family: ts-ug-mutation
Prerequisites: none. Follow-up: ts-undirectedgraph-edge-count. Complexity: O(1).
Common misconception. Adding v to the neighbours of u is enough.
An undirected edge connects u and v both ways. Storing it once makes hasEdge(v, u) false and traversals from v
miss the edge. Add both ends. Using a Set per vertex also makes repeated addEdge calls harmless.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
function addOneWayBad(g: UGraph, u: number, v: number): void {
(g.adj[u] as Set<number>).add(v);
}
Correct:
class UGraph {
adj: Array<Set<number>>;
constructor(readonly n: number) {
this.adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => new Set<number>());
}
addEdge(u: number, v: number): void {
(this.adj[u] as Set<number>).add(v);
(this.adj[v] as Set<number>).add(u);
}
hasEdge(u: number, v: number): boolean {
return (this.adj[u] as Set<number>).has(v);
}
}
The edge count is half the sum of degrees, with each self-loop added back once
ts-undirectedgraph-edge-count   lens: edge-cases   level: L2   family: ts-ug-complexity
Prerequisites: ts-undirectedgraph-edge. Follow-up: ts-undirectedgraph-components. Complexity: O(V).
Common misconception. edges = sum of neighbour counts / 2.
Each ordinary edge appears in two neighbour sets, so halving the sum works. A self-loop appears once in its own set,
so the halved sum is fractional. Add the number of loops to the sum before halving, which counts each loop as
degree two.

TypeScript Undirected Graph · 3. Core concepts (tested chunks) (continued)
