2. Overview and mental model: Doubly Linked List
A doubly linked list keeps prev and next pointers on every node, plus head and tail pointers on the list. That makes
appending, prepending and removing a known node O(1), at the cost of an extra pointer per node.
All the difficulty is in the four or six pointer updates and in the edge cases where a pointer is null: the head, the tail
and the single node. The compiler helps with null checks (DNode | null), but not with forgetting to update a link.
Pair it with a Map from key to node to build LRU caches and similar structures with O(1) operations.
Toolbox
 type DNode<T> = { value: T; prev: DNode<T> | null; next: DNode<T> | null };
const list = new DoublyLinkedList<number>();
const node = list.pushBack(1);      // returns the node, so callers can remove it later
list.pushFront(0);
removeNode(list, node);              // O(1)
3. Core concepts (tested chunks)
2 concept chunks, ordered by level. Read the misconception first and try to predict why it fails before reading the
explanation.

TypeScript Doubly Linked List: Training Data for the DSA Mentor RAG Chatbot
Page 3
Every insertion must set both directions: next on the old node and prev on the new one
ts-doublylinkedlist-core   lens: creation   level: L0   family: ts-dll-creation
Prerequisites: none. Follow-up: ts-doublylinkedlist-pop-edge. Complexity: O(1).
 Common misconception. Setting only the next links is enough, because prev can be derived when needed.
A doubly linked list stores prev and next on each node so it can be walked in both directions and a node can be
removed in O(1). If pushBack forgets to set prev, forward traversal still works but backward traversal sees nothing. A
tail pointer makes appending O(1); an empty list must set head and tail together.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 type DNode<T> = { value: T; prev: DNode<T> | null; next: DNode<T> | null };
class DllBad<T> {
  head: DNode<T> | null = null;
  tail: DNode<T> | null = null;
  pushBack(value: T): void {
    const node: DNode<T> = { value, prev: null, next: null };
    if (this.tail) this.tail.next = node;
    else this.head = node;
    this.tail = node;
  }
  toArrayReversed(): T[] {
    const out: T[] = [];
    for (let n = this.tail; n; n = n.prev) out.push(n.value);
    return out;
  }
}
Correct:
 type DNode<T> = { value: T; prev: DNode<T> | null; next: DNode<T> | null };
class DoublyLinkedList<T> {
  head: DNode<T> | null = null;
  tail: DNode<T> | null = null;
  size = 0;
  pushBack(value: T): DNode<T> {
    const node: DNode<T> = { value, prev: this.tail, next: null };
    if (this.tail) this.tail.next = node;
    else this.head = node;
    this.tail = node;
    this.size++;
    return node;
  }
  pushFront(value: T): DNode<T> {
    const node: DNode<T> = { value, prev: null, next: this.head };
    if (this.head) this.head.prev = node;
    else this.tail = node;
    this.head = node;
    this.size++;
    return node;
  }
  toArray(): T[] {
    const out: T[] = [];
    for (let n = this.head; n; n = n.next) out.push(n.value);
    return out;
  }
  toArrayReversed(): T[] {
    const out: T[] = [];
    for (let n = this.tail; n; n = n.prev) out.push(n.value);
    return out;
  }
}

TypeScript Doubly Linked List: Training Data for the DSA Mentor RAG Chatbot
Page 4
Popping the last node must clear head and tail; dereferencing a null prev crashes
ts-doublylinkedlist-pop-edge   lens: edge-cases   level: L1   family: ts-dll-delete
Prerequisites: ts-doublylinkedlist-core. Follow-up: ts-doublylinkedlist-remove-node. Complexity: O(1).
 Common misconception. popBack can always set tail = tail.prev and tail.next = null.
When the list has one node, tail.prev is null, so tail.next = null throws TypeError. The empty-list and single-node
cases must set both head and tail to null and reset the size.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 type DNode<T> = { value: T; prev: DNode<T> | null; next: DNode<T> | null };
type DLike<T> = { head: DNode<T> | null; tail: DNode<T> | null; size: number };
function popBackBad<T>(l: DLike<T>): T | undefined {
  const last = l.tail;
  if (!last) return undefined;
  l.tail = last.prev;
  l.tail!.next = null;
  l.size--;
  return last.value;
}
Correct:
 type DNode<T> = { value: T; prev: DNode<T> | null; next: DNode<T> | null };
type DLike<T> = { head: DNode<T> | null; tail: DNode<T> | null; size: number };
function popBack<T>(l: DLike<T>): T | undefined {
  const last = l.tail;
  if (!last) return undefined;
  l.tail = last.prev;
  if (l.tail) l.tail.next = null;
  else l.head = null;
  l.size--;
  return last.value;
}
