Common misconception. node.prev.next = node.next works for every node.
The first node has no prev and the last has no next, so those assignments throw TypeError. Branch on each side:
update the neighbour if there is one, otherwise move the list head or tail. Also clear the removed node links so it
cannot be reused by accident.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
type DNode<T> = { value: T; prev: DNode<T> | null; next: DNode<T> | null };
type DLike<T> = { head: DNode<T> | null; tail: DNode<T> | null; size: number };
function removeNodeBad<T>(l: DLike<T>, n: DNode<T>): void {
n.prev!.next = n.next;
n.next!.prev = n.prev;
l.size--;
}
Correct:
type DNode<T> = { value: T; prev: DNode<T> | null; next: DNode<T> | null };
type DLike<T> = { head: DNode<T> | null; tail: DNode<T> | null; size: number };
function removeNode<T>(l: DLike<T>, n: DNode<T>): void {
if (n.prev) n.prev.next = n.next;
else l.head = n.next;
if (n.next) n.next.prev = n.prev;
else l.tail = n.prev;
n.prev = n.next = null;
l.size--;
}
An LRU cache pairs a hash map with a doubly linked list so reads and evictions are O(1)
ts-doublylinkedlist-lru   lens: patterns   level: L3   family: ts-dll-patterns
Prerequisites: ts-doublylinkedlist-reverse. Follow-up: none. Complexity: O(1).
Common misconception. A Map alone gives O(1) recency updates for any key type.
A Map can track recency through insertion order, but a hand-built LRU with a doubly linked list and a Map from key to
node is the classic design for interviews and for languages without ordered maps. On get, unlink the node and push it
to the front; on put, insert at the front and evict the tail when full. A get that does not move the node makes the cache
FIFO.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):

